INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Anaemia — Approach & IDA Management','beginner','flashcard','What are the WHO criteria for diagnosing anaemia in adults?','["WHO defines anaemia as: Hb <13 g/dL in adult men; Hb <12 g/dL in non-pregnant adult women; Hb <11 g/dL in pregnant women. Severity: mild 10-11.9 g/dL (women)/10-12.9 g/dL (men); moderate 8-9.9 g/dL; severe <8 g/dL; very severe <6.5 g/dL."]','A','Mnemonic: 13-12-11 (men, women, pregnant). Severity cut-offs guide transfusion decisions.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Anaemia — Approach & IDA Management','beginner','flashcard','What iron studies confirm iron deficiency anaemia (IDA)?','["IDA iron study pattern: 1. Serum ferritin LOW (<15 mcg/L; <30 mcg/L if concurrent inflammation) — most sensitive store marker. 2. Serum iron LOW. 3. TIBC/transferrin HIGH (>70 mcmol/L). 4. Transferrin saturation LOW (<20%). 5. Blood film: microcytic hypochromic red cells, pencil cells, anisocytosis."]','A','Ferritin is an acute phase reactant — can be falsely normal in inflammation. Low ferritin is always pathological; normal/high ferritin does not exclude IDA if inflammatory markers are raised.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Anaemia — Approach & IDA Management','intermediate','flashcard','What is the first-line oral iron treatment for IDA per Ghana STG and what is the dose?','["First-line: Ferrous sulphate 200 mg (65 mg elemental iron) THREE times daily (TDS) orally. Duration: continue for 3 months after Hb normalises to replenish stores. Take on empty stomach for best absorption; ascorbic acid (vitamin C) enhances absorption. Avoid with tea, calcium, antacids, PPIs."]','A','Ghana STG recommends ferrous sulphate 200mg TDS. The extra 3 months beyond Hb correction is crucial — stopping early leads to relapse as stores remain depleted.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Anaemia — Approach & IDA Management','intermediate','flashcard','What are the indications for intravenous (IV) iron therapy in IDA?','["IV iron indications: 1. Intolerance or non-compliance with oral iron. 2. Malabsorption (coeliac disease, IBD, post-gastrectomy). 3. Rapid repletion needed (severe symptomatic anaemia, pre-operative). 4. Haemodialysis patients on erythropoietin. 5. Chronic kidney disease. 6. Ongoing blood loss exceeding oral replacement capacity. Formulations: ferric carboxymaltose, iron sucrose, low-molecular-weight iron dextran."]','A','IV iron bypasses the gut — ideal in malabsorption and IBD. Ferric carboxymaltose allows high-dose single infusion. Always have resuscitation available for anaphylaxis risk.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Anaemia — Approach & IDA Management','intermediate','flashcard','What reticulocyte response is expected after starting iron therapy for IDA, and when does Hb normalise?','["Expected response to iron therapy: 1. Reticulocytosis peaks at 7-10 days after starting treatment. 2. Hb rises ~1-2 g/dL per week. 3. Hb normalises in 4-8 weeks. 4. Full store replenishment takes 3 additional months. Failure to respond: consider wrong diagnosis, non-compliance, ongoing blood loss, malabsorption, or dual deficiency (B12/folate)."]','A','A reticulocyte peak at day 7-10 is the earliest sign of response — it confirms the diagnosis of IDA and adequate treatment. No reticulocytosis by day 14 warrants reassessment.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Anaemia — Approach & IDA Management','beginner','flashcard','How do you approach a patient with microcytic hypochromic anaemia on blood film?','["Microcytic hypochromic anaemia approach: 1. Iron studies — ferritin, serum iron, TIBC, transferrin saturation. 2. If IDA confirmed — find the SOURCE of iron deficiency (dietary, blood loss GI/menstrual, malabsorption). 3. If iron studies normal/high — consider thalassaemia (Hb electrophoresis), sideroblastic anaemia (bone marrow), anaemia of chronic disease. 4. Hb electrophoresis if thalassaemia trait suspected. 5. Treat underlying cause AND replace iron."]','A','Differential for microcytic anaemia: IDA, thalassaemia, anaemia of chronic disease (late), sideroblastic anaemia — mnemonic TAILS (Thalassaemia, Anaemia of CD, IDA, Lead, Sideroblastic).','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Anaemia — Approach & IDA Management','advanced','flashcard','What are the clinical features of severe iron deficiency beyond anaemia?','["Extra-haematological features of severe IDA: 1. Koilonychia (spoon-shaped nails). 2. Angular stomatitis and glossitis. 3. Dysphagia — Plummer-Vinson (Patterson-Kelly) syndrome (post-cricoid web). 4. Pica — craving for non-food items (ice, soil, clay). 5. Restless leg syndrome. 6. Hair loss. 7. Impaired cognitive function and fatigue disproportionate to Hb level. 8. Reduced exercise tolerance."]','A','Plummer-Vinson syndrome (IDA + dysphagia + oesophageal web) is associated with increased risk of postcricoid carcinoma — endoscopy is mandatory if present.','universal',false,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Anaemia — Approach & IDA Management','intermediate','flashcard','When should red cell transfusion be given in anaemia, and what are the triggers?','["Transfusion triggers in anaemia: 1. Acute symptomatic anaemia: Hb <7 g/dL in stable patients (target 7-9 g/dL post-transfusion). 2. Cardiac disease: Hb <8 g/dL or symptomatic at higher Hb. 3. Active bleeding with haemodynamic instability — regardless of Hb. 4. Symptomatic anaemia at ANY Hb (chest pain, severe dyspnoea, altered consciousness). 5. Pre-operative if Hb <8 g/dL for major surgery. Each unit of packed red cells raises Hb by ~1 g/dL."]','A','Restrictive transfusion strategy (trigger Hb 7 g/dL) is as safe as liberal (9 g/dL) in most patients and uses fewer resources — key principle in resource-limited settings.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Anaemia — Approach & IDA Management','advanced','flashcard','How does anaemia of chronic disease (ACD) differ from IDA in iron studies and management?','["ACD vs IDA iron studies: ACD — ferritin NORMAL or HIGH, serum iron LOW, TIBC LOW/normal, transferrin saturation low-normal. IDA — ferritin LOW, serum iron LOW, TIBC HIGH, transferrin saturation LOW. ACD mechanism: hepcidin blocks iron release from macrophages and absorption. Management: treat underlying disease; EPO in renal anaemia; IV iron if functional iron deficiency component; transfuse only if severely symptomatic."]','A','Key distinction: TIBC is LOW in ACD (liver makes less transferrin as acute phase response) but HIGH in IDA (liver upregulates transferrin when iron-starved). Ferritin differentiates: low = IDA, high = ACD.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Anaemia — Approach & IDA Management','intermediate','flashcard','What is the approach to normocytic anaemia and its common causes?','["Normocytic anaemia approach: 1. Check reticulocyte count — HIGH reticulocytes suggest haemolysis or acute blood loss; LOW suggests bone marrow failure. 2. Common causes: acute blood loss, haemolytic anaemia, aplastic anaemia, CKD, hypothyroidism, mixed deficiency (IDA + B12/folate), early IDA, AML/MDS. 3. Investigations: reticulocyte count, LDH, bilirubin, haptoglobin (haemolysis screen), blood film, renal/thyroid function, bone marrow biopsy if pancytopenia. 4. Treat underlying cause."]','A','The reticulocyte count is the key branch point in normocytic anaemia — it tells you whether the marrow is responding (high = peripheral destruction/loss) or failing (low = production problem).','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','B12/Folate Deficiency Management','beginner','flashcard','What are the neurological features specific to vitamin B12 deficiency that do NOT occur in folate deficiency?','["Neurological features of B12 deficiency (not folate): Subacute Combined Degeneration of the Spinal Cord (SACD) — affects: 1. Posterior columns: loss of vibration sense and proprioception (sensory ataxia, positive Romberg). 2. Lateral corticospinal tracts: upper motor neurone signs (spasticity, hyperreflexia, upgoing plantars). 3. Peripheral neuropathy: numbness, tingling (glove-and-stocking). 4. Cognitive impairment / dementia. 5. Optic atrophy. NEVER give folate alone in combined deficiency — can precipitate/worsen neurological damage."]','A','Critical rule: NEVER correct folate without also correcting B12 in combined deficiency — folate drives haemopoiesis and can precipitate or worsen SACD by depleting remaining B12 stores faster.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','B12/Folate Deficiency Management','intermediate','flashcard','What is the IM hydroxocobalamin regimen for pernicious anaemia — both loading and maintenance?','["IM hydroxocobalamin for pernicious anaemia (and other B12 deficiency with neurological involvement): Loading: 1 mg IM on alternate days for 2 weeks (total 7 doses) — PLUS if neurological features present, continue alternate-day dosing until no further improvement. Maintenance: 1 mg IM every 3 MONTHS for LIFE. Note: Cyanocobalamin can also be used but hydroxocobalamin preferred (longer half-life, retained better). Oral B12 is NOT adequate for pernicious anaemia (intrinsic factor absent)."]','A','Pernicious anaemia = autoimmune destruction of gastric parietal cells → no intrinsic factor → cannot absorb dietary B12 → requires lifelong IM replacement. 1mg every 3 months forever.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','B12/Folate Deficiency Management','intermediate','flashcard','When is oral high-dose B12 appropriate instead of IM injections?','["Oral high-dose B12 is appropriate when: 1. Dietary deficiency (vegans, strict vegetarians) — no intrinsic factor problem, absorption intact. 2. Metformin-induced B12 malabsorption — oral high-dose often sufficient. 3. Patient preference/needle phobia where absorption is intact. 4. Post-ileal resection — small amount of B12 absorbed by passive diffusion (1-2% of dose) at high oral doses. Dose: 1000-2000 mcg orally daily. NOT appropriate for: pernicious anaemia, gastrectomy, severe ileal disease, where gut absorption is fundamentally impaired."]','A','1% of oral B12 is absorbed by passive diffusion without intrinsic factor — so 1000-2000 mcg/day orally delivers ~10-20 mcg, which is adequate for non-PA deficiency. Passive diffusion is the pharmacological basis.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','B12/Folate Deficiency Management','beginner','flashcard','What is the standard oral folic acid dose for treating folate deficiency?','["Folic acid treatment: Standard dose: 5 mg orally DAILY for 4 months (or until the underlying cause is corrected). Prevention of neural tube defects: 400 mcg (0.4 mg) daily preconceptionally and in first trimester; HIGH RISK (previous NTD, anti-epileptics, diabetes, BMI >30): 5 mg daily. Maintenance in ongoing haemolysis/increased turnover (e.g., sickle cell, thalassaemia): 5 mg daily ongoing. Methotrexate toxicity rescue: folinic acid (not folic acid)."]','A','Treatment dose = 5mg/day. Prevention dose = 400mcg/day (low risk) or 5mg/day (high risk). Folinic acid (not folic acid) rescues methotrexate toxicity — folic acid cannot bypass DHFR inhibition.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','B12/Folate Deficiency Management','beginner','flashcard','What blood film and haematological findings are seen in megaloblastic anaemia?','["Megaloblastic anaemia findings: Blood film: 1. Macro-ovalocytes (oval macrocytes). 2. Hypersegmented neutrophils (>5 lobes in >5% neutrophils, or any neutrophil with ≥6 lobes). 3. Pancytopenia in severe cases. Indices: MCV raised (macrocytosis >100 fL), MCV can be very high (>120 fL). Bone marrow: hypercellular with megaloblastic changes, giant metamyelocytes. LDH and bilirubin raised (ineffective erythropoiesis/intramedullary haemolysis)."]','A','Hypersegmented neutrophil is the EARLIEST blood film finding in B12/folate deficiency — appears before macrocytosis. A neutrophil with ≥6 lobes is always pathological and specific for megaloblastic process.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','B12/Folate Deficiency Management','intermediate','flashcard','What are the common causes of vitamin B12 deficiency and how do you distinguish them?','["Causes of B12 deficiency: 1. Pernicious anaemia — autoimmune; anti-intrinsic factor antibodies (specific, 50-70% sensitive); anti-parietal cell antibodies (sensitive but not specific); Schilling test historically used. 2. Dietary — vegans, strict vegetarians; no animal products. 3. Gastrectomy/bariatric surgery — loss of parietal cells/intrinsic factor. 4. Ileal disease/resection — terminal ileum absorbs B12-IF complex. 5. Metformin — reduces absorption (mechanism unclear, possibly ileal). 6. Prolonged PPI use — reduces gastric acid needed for B12 release from food. 7. Blind loop syndrome — bacterial consumption."]','A','Pernicious anaemia is the most common cause in the UK/Ghana. Anti-intrinsic factor antibody is specific but only 50-70% sensitive — a negative result does not exclude PA. Treat and investigate concurrently.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','B12/Folate Deficiency Management','advanced','flashcard','What haematological emergency can occur when starting B12/folate treatment, and how is it prevented?','["Hypokalaemia on starting treatment: Rapid erythropoiesis after B12/folate replacement drives potassium into new red cells → sudden fall in serum K+. Risk: cardiac arrhythmias, especially in severe megaloblastic anaemia. Prevention/management: 1. Monitor serum K+ closely in first few days of treatment. 2. Oral or IV potassium supplementation if K+ falls. 3. Cardiac monitoring in severe cases. 4. Avoid rapid transfusion in severe megaloblastic anaemia (can precipitate heart failure — bone marrow will respond rapidly to replacement)."]','A','Hypokalaemia in megaloblastic anaemia treatment is a rare but dangerous complication. The sudden bone marrow activation consumes potassium for new cell synthesis. Monitor K+ at 24-48h after starting therapy.','universal',false,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','B12/Folate Deficiency Management','intermediate','flashcard','How does subacute combined degeneration of the cord present and how is it investigated?','["SACD clinical features: 1. Onset: insidious, weeks-months. 2. Symmetrical sensory symptoms first: tingling/numbness hands and feet. 3. Sensory ataxia: Romberg positive, stamping gait. 4. Upper motor neurone signs: spasticity, hyperreflexia, upgoing plantar responses. 5. In late stages: cognitive decline, optic neuropathy. Investigations: Serum B12 (may be low-normal — methylmalonic acid and homocysteine more sensitive); MRI spine: T2 hyperintensity in posterior/lateral columns; anti-IF antibodies; full blood count (macrocytosis may be absent if mixed deficiency)."]','A','MRI cord in SACD shows characteristic posterior column T2 signal — the inverted V sign on axial imaging. Normal Hb/MCV does NOT exclude B12 deficiency causing neurological damage — always check B12 in unexplained myelopathy.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','B12/Folate Deficiency Management','advanced','flashcard','What is the role of methylmalonic acid (MMA) and homocysteine in diagnosing B12 deficiency?','["MMA and homocysteine in B12 deficiency: Both are elevated in B12 deficiency (B12 is a cofactor for reactions that clear both). Methylmalonic acid (MMA): ELEVATED in B12 deficiency; normal in folate deficiency — most specific for true tissue B12 deficiency. Homocysteine: ELEVATED in BOTH B12 AND folate deficiency — not specific. Use when: serum B12 is borderline (150-300 pmol/L) and clinical suspicion remains high. Elevated MMA confirms functional B12 deficiency even with normal serum B12."]','A','MMA is the KEY discriminator: elevated MMA = B12 deficient (folate deficiency does NOT raise MMA). Homocysteine elevated in both. This distinction matters when serum B12 is borderline — check MMA to confirm.','universal',false,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','B12/Folate Deficiency Management','intermediate','flashcard','What are the causes of macrocytosis WITHOUT megaloblastic change on bone marrow?','["Non-megaloblastic macrocytosis causes: 1. Alcohol excess — most common non-nutritional cause (direct effect on RBC membrane). 2. Liver disease — increased lipid deposition in RBC membrane. 3. Hypothyroidism. 4. Reticulocytosis (reticulocytes are larger — haemolysis, haemorrhage). 5. Aplastic anaemia. 6. Myelodysplastic syndrome (MDS). 7. Drugs — hydroxycarbamide (hydroxyurea), azathioprine, zidovudine (AZT). 8. Pregnancy. Key: bone marrow in non-megaloblastic macrocytosis does NOT show hypersegmented neutrophils/megaloblasts."]','A','Alcohol is the most common cause of macrocytosis in many populations. It causes macrocytosis even without folate deficiency — direct toxic effect on erythropoiesis. MCV normalises in 3 months of abstinence.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Haemolytic Anaemia — Clinical Management','beginner','flashcard','What are the clinical and laboratory features of haemolytic anaemia?','["Haemolytic anaemia features: Clinical: pallor, jaundice (unconjugated hyperbilirubinaemia), splenomegaly, dark urine (haemoglobinuria in intravascular haemolysis), gallstones (pigment stones from chronic haemolysis). Laboratory: Hb LOW; reticulocyte count HIGH (>2%); LDH ELEVATED; unconjugated bilirubin ELEVATED; haptoglobin LOW (haptoglobin binds free Hb and is consumed); blood film: spherocytes (AIHA, HS), sickle cells, schistocytes (microangiopathic); direct antiglobulin test (DAT/Coombs) positive in immune haemolysis."]','A','Haemolysis triad: raised LDH + raised unconjugated bilirubin + LOW haptoglobin. Haptoglobin is consumed binding free haemoglobin — low haptoglobin is the most sensitive marker of intravascular haemolysis.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Haemolytic Anaemia — Clinical Management','intermediate','flashcard','How is autoimmune haemolytic anaemia (AIHA) classified and what is the first-line treatment?','["AIHA classification and treatment: Warm AIHA (IgG, reacts at 37°C — most common, ~70%): causes: idiopathic, CLL, lymphoma, SLE, drugs (methyldopa, penicillin). Treatment: 1st line — prednisolone 1 mg/kg/day; response in 1-3 weeks; taper over months. Refractory: rituximab, splenectomy, azathioprine. Cold AIHA (IgM, reacts at <37°C): causes: Mycoplasma, EBV, lymphoma. Treatment: keep warm, treat underlying cause, rituximab; steroids NOT effective in cold AIHA. Mixed AIHA: both warm and cold antibodies — poor prognosis; rituximab preferred."]','A','Key distinction: steroids WORK in warm AIHA (IgG) but do NOT work in cold AIHA (IgM/complement mediated). Giving steroids for cold AIHA is a common mistake. Rituximab (anti-CD20) is effective in both.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Haemolytic Anaemia — Clinical Management','intermediate','flashcard','What are the indications for splenectomy in haemolytic anaemia and what precautions are required?','["Splenectomy indications in haemolytic anaemia: 1. Hereditary spherocytosis (HS) — moderate/severe HS; curative (removes site of RBC destruction); defer until age >6 to preserve immune function. 2. Warm AIHA — refractory to steroids and rituximab; 2nd/3rd line. 3. Haemolytic anaemia with hypersplenism causing massive splenomegaly. Pre-splenectomy vaccines (at least 2 weeks before, ideally 4-6 weeks): 1. Pneumococcal (PCV13 + PPSV23). 2. Meningococcal (ACWY + B). 3. Haemophilus influenzae type b (Hib). Post-splenectomy: lifelong penicillin V prophylaxis (or amoxicillin/erythromycin)."]','A','OPSI (Overwhelming Post-Splenectomy Infection) — encapsulated organisms (Strep pneumoniae, H. influenzae, N. meningitidis) — can kill within 24h. Vaccines + lifelong penicillin are MANDATORY. Never miss vaccination.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Haemolytic Anaemia — Clinical Management','intermediate','flashcard','What vaccines must be given before splenectomy and when should they be administered?','["Pre-splenectomy vaccination schedule: Timing: ideally 4-6 weeks before surgery (minimum 2 weeks). Vaccines required: 1. Pneumococcal conjugate vaccine (PCV13/PCV15) + polysaccharide vaccine (PPSV23) — 8 weeks apart. 2. Meningococcal ACWY conjugate vaccine. 3. Meningococcal B vaccine (2 doses). 4. Haemophilus influenzae type b (Hib) conjugate vaccine. 5. Annual influenza vaccine. Post-splenectomy: booster PPSV23 every 5 years; lifelong prophylactic penicillin V 250-500mg BD; medical alert bracelet; seek immediate care for febrile illness (treat as emergency)."]','A','Mnemonic for post-splenectomy bugs: SHiN — Strep pneumoniae (most common, most deadly), H. influenzae, Neisseria meningitidis. All are encapsulated organisms requiring opsonisation by splenic macrophages.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Haemolytic Anaemia — Clinical Management','intermediate','flashcard','How is hereditary spherocytosis (HS) diagnosed and what are the management options?','["Hereditary spherocytosis diagnosis: Blood film: spherocytes (dense, round, no central pallor) + raised MCHC. Osmotic fragility test: increased fragility (historical). EMA (eosin-5-maleimide) binding test: REDUCED — preferred modern diagnostic test; measures band 3 protein. Family history (AD inheritance, 75%). Management: 1. Mild HS: folic acid 5mg daily (increased folate demand); monitor. 2. Moderate-severe HS: splenectomy (curative — removes destruction site, not the defect). 3. Aplastic crisis: parvovirus B19 infection — transfusion support, resolves spontaneously. 4. Regular folate supplementation in all."]','A','EMA binding test has replaced osmotic fragility for diagnosing HS — more sensitive and specific, less influenced by storage. Parvovirus B19 causes aplastic crisis in HS by infecting erythroid progenitors — Hb can drop precipitously.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Haemolytic Anaemia — Clinical Management','advanced','flashcard','What is the management of a haemolytic transfusion reaction?','["Acute haemolytic transfusion reaction (ABO incompatibility) management: STOP transfusion immediately. 1. Call for help; maintain IV access. 2. Send: blood and urine for free Hb, repeat crossmatch, DAT, repeat group and screen, FBC, clotting, U&E, LFTs. 3. IV fluids — aggressive hydration to maintain urine output >100 mL/h (prevent renal tubular haemoglobin damage). 4. Monitor for: DIC (treat with FFP/platelets/cryo), acute renal failure (may need dialysis), hyperkalaemia, hypotension. 5. Inform blood bank immediately. 6. Document and report as serious adverse event."]','A','Acute haemolytic reaction is usually ABO mismatch — often clerical error. First sign: fever, rigors, back/loin pain, haemoglobinuria. STOP transfusion first — even if reaction is mild (could be the start of a severe reaction).','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Haemolytic Anaemia — Clinical Management','advanced','flashcard','What is microangiopathic haemolytic anaemia (MAHA) and what conditions cause it?','["MAHA: mechanical destruction of red cells by fibrin strands in small vessels → schistocytes on film. Causes: 1. TTP (Thrombotic Thrombocytopenic Purpura) — ADAMTS13 deficiency; pentad: MAHA + thrombocytopenia + fever + neurological + renal; treat with PLASMA EXCHANGE urgently. 2. HUS (Haemolytic Uraemic Syndrome) — usually post-Shiga toxin E. coli O157:H7; triad: MAHA + thrombocytopenia + AKI; supportive treatment; NO antibiotics (worsen toxin release). 3. DIC. 4. Pre-eclampsia/HELLP syndrome. 5. Malignant hypertension. 6. Prosthetic heart valves. Schistocytes on film = MAHA until proven otherwise."]','A','TTP vs HUS: TTP = neurological predominant, treat with plasma exchange; HUS = renal predominant, supportive only. Schistocytes (helmet cells, fragmented RBCs) are the hallmark of MAHA on blood film.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Haemolytic Anaemia — Clinical Management','intermediate','flashcard','What is G6PD deficiency and how is an acute haemolytic crisis managed?','["G6PD deficiency: X-linked; most common enzyme deficiency; protects against oxidative stress. Triggers of haemolytic crisis: infections (most common), drugs (primaquine, dapsone, nitrofurantoin, rasburicase), fava beans, naphthalene. Crisis features: acute intravascular haemolysis — jaundice, dark urine (haemoglobinuria), abdominal/back pain, rapid Hb fall. Blood film: bite cells, blister cells, Heinz bodies (supravital stain). Management: 1. REMOVE trigger. 2. IV fluids — maintain urine output. 3. Transfuse if severe symptomatic anaemia (Hb <7 g/dL or rapidly falling). 4. Folic acid. 5. Avoid offending agents lifelong. NB: G6PD levels normal during acute crisis (enzyme-deficient cells haemolysed first)."]','A','Test G6PD level AFTER the crisis resolves — during haemolysis the most deficient cells are destroyed and remaining cells have higher G6PD, giving falsely normal result. Test 3 months post-crisis for accurate diagnosis.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Haemolytic Anaemia — Clinical Management','advanced','flashcard','What is paroxysmal nocturnal haemoglobinuria (PNH) and how is it diagnosed and treated?','["PNH: acquired clonal disorder — somatic PIG-A mutation → loss of GPI-anchored proteins (CD55, CD59) → uncontrolled complement activation → intravascular haemolysis. Clinical: haemolytic anaemia, venous thrombosis (unusual sites: hepatic vein — Budd-Chiari, cerebral), cytopenias, smooth muscle dysfunction (dysphagia, erectile dysfunction). Diagnosis: FLOW CYTOMETRY — absence of CD55/CD59 on red cells and granulocytes. Treatment: eculizumab (C5 complement inhibitor) — dramatically reduces haemolysis and thrombosis; meningococcal vaccine mandatory before eculizumab; allogeneic SCT curative; anticoagulation for thrombotic events; folic acid."]','A','PNH is the only haemolytic anaemia that causes venous thrombosis — paradoxically haemolysis activates platelets and complement drives thrombosis. Classic triad: haemolytic anaemia + thrombosis + cytopenias. Flow cytometry for GPI-anchored proteins is diagnostic.','universal',false,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Haemolytic Anaemia — Clinical Management','intermediate','flashcard','What are the transfusion triggers and targets in chronic haemolytic anaemia?','["Transfusion in chronic haemolytic anaemia: Trigger for acute transfusion: Hb <7 g/dL with symptoms OR rapid fall in Hb regardless of absolute value. Symptomatic at higher Hb (cardiac disease): transfuse at Hb <8-9 g/dL. Aplastic crisis (parvovirus B19): transfuse to keep Hb at safe level until marrow recovers (~2 weeks). Long-term transfusion (thalassaemia major, severe SCD): pre-transfusion Hb target 9-10 g/dL; post-transfusion 13-14 g/dL. Complications of chronic transfusion: alloimmunisation (most problematic in SCD — up to 30%), iron overload (start chelation when ferritin >1000 mcg/L or after 10-20 units)."]','A','Alloimmunisation is the major complication of chronic transfusion in SCD — occurs in up to 30% (vs 3% in non-SCD). Extended red cell antigen matching (Rh, Kell, Kidd, Duffy systems) recommended for SCD patients.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Sickle Cell Disease — Clinical Management','beginner','flashcard','What is the stepwise analgesia ladder for managing acute sickle cell pain crisis?','["Sickle cell pain crisis analgesia ladder: Step 1 (mild pain): 1. Paracetamol 1g QDS. 2. NSAIDs (ibuprofen/diclofenac) — if renal function adequate. 3. Oral hydration 2-3L/day. Step 2 (moderate pain): Add weak opioids — codeine 30-60mg QDS or tramadol. Step 3 (severe pain): Strong opioids — morphine IV/SC (0.1 mg/kg bolus, then PCA or regular dosing); intranasal diamorphine for rapid effect while IV access obtained. All steps: 1. IV fluids if cannot tolerate oral. 2. Oxygen if SpO2 <95%. 3. Warmth. 4. Anti-emetics. 5. Anxiolytics if required. Target: analgesia within 30 minutes of presentation."]','A','SCD pain crisis analgesia — aim for adequate analgesia within 30 minutes. Do NOT under-treat (patients often under-dosed). Opioid-based analgesia is the cornerstone for severe crisis. NSAIDs add opioid-sparing effect.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Sickle Cell Disease — Clinical Management','intermediate','flashcard','How does hydroxyurea (hydroxycarbamide) work in sickle cell disease and what is the dose?','["Hydroxyurea in SCD: Mechanism: induces production of fetal haemoglobin (HbF) by reactivating gamma-globin gene → HbF inhibits HbS polymerisation → reduces sickling. Additional effects: reduces WBC and platelets (anti-inflammatory), reduces adhesion molecules, increases red cell water content. Benefits: reduces painful crises by ~50%, reduces acute chest syndrome, reduces transfusion requirement, reduces stroke risk, may extend survival. Dose: starting dose 15 mg/kg/day orally; increase by 5 mg/kg every 8-12 weeks; maximum 35 mg/kg/day; titrate to maximum tolerated dose. Monitor: FBC every 4-8 weeks (myelosuppression); target MCV >100 fL (indicates HbF induction)."]','A','HbF effect: HbF does not participate in deoxygenated HbS polymer formation — even 20-30% HbF substantially reduces sickling. A rising MCV on hydroxyurea is a proxy marker of increasing HbF. Myelosuppression is dose-limiting.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Sickle Cell Disease — Clinical Management','advanced','flashcard','What are the indications for exchange transfusion in sickle cell disease?','["Exchange transfusion indications in SCD: Acute (emergency): 1. Acute stroke — reduce HbS to <30% rapidly. 2. Acute chest syndrome (ACS) not responding to simple transfusion. 3. Multi-organ failure. 4. Priapism lasting >4-6 hours not responding to conservative management. 5. Severe sequestration crisis. Simple transfusion may be used first if exchange not immediately available. Chronic (prophylactic): 1. Secondary stroke prevention (monthly exchange, maintain HbS <30%). 2. Primary stroke prevention in children with abnormal TCD (transcranial Doppler >200 cm/s). 3. Recurrent ACS despite hydroxyurea. 4. Pregnancy with severe disease. Exchange vs simple: exchange avoids iron overload and hyperviscosity."]','A','Exchange transfusion rapidly lowers HbS% without raising total Hb (avoids hyperviscosity). For stroke: reduce HbS to <30% within hours. Simple top-up transfusion risks hyperviscosity if Hb rises too high in SCD.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Sickle Cell Disease — Clinical Management','intermediate','flashcard','What is acute chest syndrome (ACS) in SCD and how is it managed?','["Acute Chest Syndrome (ACS): Definition: new pulmonary infiltrate on CXR + at least one: fever, chest pain, dyspnoea, cough, hypoxia. Most common cause of death in SCD. Causes: infection (most common in children — Chlamydia, Mycoplasma, S. pneumoniae), fat embolism from infarcted bone marrow, in-situ sickling, hypoventilation. Management: 1. Oxygen (target SpO2 ≥95%). 2. IV fluids (avoid overhydration — worsens pulmonary oedema). 3. Analgesia (adequate — splinting worsens hypoventilation). 4. Incentive spirometry. 5. Broad-spectrum antibiotics (cover atypicals — azithromycin + beta-lactam). 6. Bronchodilators if wheeze. 7. Simple transfusion (Hb <9 g/dL) or exchange transfusion if severe/deteriorating. 8. ITU if deteriorating."]','A','ACS is the leading cause of death in SCD. Key intervention: transfusion (simple or exchange) to reduce HbS concentration and improve oxygenation. Atypical cover is essential as Mycoplasma/Chlamydia are common pathogens in ACS.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Sickle Cell Disease — Clinical Management','intermediate','flashcard','What antibiotic prophylaxis and vaccination does a child with sickle cell disease require?','["SCD prophylaxis: Antibiotic prophylaxis: Penicillin V (phenoxymethylpenicillin) from birth/diagnosis: dose birth-3 years: 62.5mg BD; 3-5 years: 125mg BD; >5 years: 250mg BD. Continue for life (functional asplenia from repeated splenic infarction by age 5). Penicillin allergy: erythromycin. Vaccinations (enhanced schedule): 1. Standard childhood vaccines + boosters. 2. Pneumococcal — PCV13 primary course + PPSV23 at age 2 and boosters every 5 years. 3. Meningococcal ACWY + MenB. 4. Hib. 5. Annual influenza. 6. Hepatitis B (if multiple transfusions). Rationale: functional asplenia → risk of OPSI with encapsulated organisms."]','A','SCD children are functionally asplenic from infancy due to repeated splenic infarction (autosplenectomy). Penicillin from birth dramatically reduces S. pneumoniae mortality — one of the most impactful interventions in SCD.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Sickle Cell Disease — Clinical Management','advanced','flashcard','What is splenic sequestration crisis in SCD and how is it managed?','["Splenic sequestration crisis: Definition: rapid pooling of blood in spleen → massive splenomegaly, rapidly falling Hb (>2 g/dL fall), thrombocytopenia, hypovolaemia. Age: typically children <5 years (before autosplenectomy). Features: left-sided abdominal pain, rapid abdominal distension, pallor, cardiovascular collapse. Management: 1. URGENT IV access + fluid resuscitation. 2. Blood transfusion (red cells) — cautiously: aim to restore circulating volume; overly aggressive transfusion can cause hyperviscosity when sequestered blood re-enters circulation. 3. Target Hb rise of ~2 g/dL initially. 4. Monitor closely after transfusion. 5. Splenectomy after recovery to prevent recurrence (recurs in 50%). 6. Chronic transfusion programme as bridge to splenectomy in young children."]','A','Splenic sequestration: blood is TRAPPED in the spleen, not destroyed — Hb falls rapidly, spleen enlarges acutely. When transfused, be cautious: the trapped blood eventually re-enters circulation and total Hb can overshoot, causing hyperviscosity.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Sickle Cell Disease — Clinical Management','advanced','flashcard','What is the role of allogeneic stem cell transplantation in sickle cell disease?','["Allogeneic SCT in SCD: Currently the ONLY curative treatment for SCD. Indications (high-risk features justifying SCT): 1. Stroke or TIA. 2. Recurrent ACS. 3. Recurrent severe pain crises (≥3/year) despite hydroxyurea. 4. Nephropathy. 5. Pulmonary hypertension. Best results: HLA-identical sibling donor (80-90% cure rate); matched unrelated donor increasingly used. Age: best outcomes in children <16 years. Limitations: donor availability (~25% have HLA-matched sibling), transplant-related mortality (5-10%), GvHD risk. Gene therapy: emerging — autologous stem cells modified to express HbF or anti-sickling beta-globin; lentiviral or CRISPR-based approaches in trials."]','A','SCT is curative but currently limited by donor availability and transplant risk. Gene therapy trials (e.g., betibeglogene — LentiGlobin) have shown >90% reduction in VOCs — may become the curative treatment of the future.','universal',false,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Sickle Cell Disease — Clinical Management','intermediate','flashcard','What are the long-term organ complications of sickle cell disease and how are they monitored?','["SCD long-term complications and monitoring: 1. Stroke/cerebrovascular disease: annual TCD (transcranial Doppler) in children 2-16 years; MRI brain if TCD abnormal. 2. Nephropathy: annual urine ACR, eGFR; ACE inhibitors for microalbuminuria. 3. Pulmonary hypertension: annual echocardiogram; referral if TRV >2.5 m/s. 4. Avascular necrosis (femoral/humeral head): X-ray/MRI of symptomatic joints. 5. Retinopathy: annual ophthalmology screening from age 10. 6. Leg ulcers: wound care, zinc supplements, avoid trauma. 7. Priapism: education, self-management, urology referral. 8. Cardiomegaly: ECG, echo."]','A','SCD is a systemic vasculopathy — virtually every organ is at risk. Structured annual review covers renal, cardiac, pulmonary, neurological and ophthalmological systems. Early detection allows intervention before irreversible damage.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Sickle Cell Disease — Clinical Management','intermediate','flashcard','How do you manage a priapism in sickle cell disease?','["SCD priapism management: Definition: prolonged painful penile erection >4 hours — ischaemic (most common in SCD) vs non-ischaemic. First aid (0-4 hours): 1. Oral analgesics (paracetamol ± opioids). 2. Oral/IV hydration. 3. Warm bath. 4. Light exercise (walking may terminate). 5. Urinate. Escalation (4-6+ hours, if above fails): 1. Urology review urgently. 2. Aspiration and irrigation of corpus cavernosum. 3. Intracavernosal phenylephrine (alpha-agonist — vasoconstriction). 4. Exchange transfusion (reduces HbS%). 5. Surgical shunt if >12 hours (prevents erectile dysfunction). Long-term prevention: hydroxyurea, phosphodiesterase-5 inhibitors (sildenafil — prophylaxis only, NOT during acute episode)."]','A','Priapism >6 hours causes irreversible ischaemic damage to erectile tissue → permanent erectile dysfunction. Aspiration + phenylephrine is the key urological intervention. Sildenafil is for PROPHYLAXIS only — giving it during acute ischaemic priapism is contraindicated.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Sickle Cell Disease — Clinical Management','beginner','flashcard','What are the precipitants of a sickle cell pain crisis and how are they avoided?','["SCD pain crisis precipitants (triggers): 1. Infection/fever — most common trigger; any febrile illness. 2. Cold — vasoconstriction. 3. Dehydration — increases HbS concentration and blood viscosity. 4. Hypoxia — deoxygenation promotes HbS polymerisation. 5. Exercise/exertion — strenuous physical activity. 6. Alcohol — dehydration. 7. Stress (emotional and physical). 8. Altitude — reduced pO2. 9. Menstruation. Prevention: stay warm; maintain hydration (3L/day); avoid strenuous exercise; prompt treatment of infections; penicillin prophylaxis; influenza and pneumococcal vaccination; hydroxyurea; patient education."]','A','Deoxygenation is the key molecular trigger — HbS polymerises when deoxygenated. Anything that reduces O2 delivery (hypoxia, cold, dehydration, low flow states) promotes sickling. Hydration is the simplest preventive measure.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Thalassaemia — Clinical Management','beginner','flashcard','What is the regular transfusion regimen for thalassaemia major and what are the targets?','["Thalassaemia major transfusion programme: Frequency: every 3-4 WEEKS (28-35 days). Pre-transfusion Hb target: 9-10 g/dL (some centres 9.5-10.5 g/dL). Post-transfusion Hb target: 13-14 g/dL (avoid exceeding 14-15 g/dL — hyperviscosity risk). Rationale: suppresses ineffective erythropoiesis (prevents extramedullary haemopoiesis, bony deformities, splenomegaly), maintains adequate Hb for growth and development. Red cell specification: leucodepleted, CMV-negative (if CMV status unknown at start); extended phenotype matched (Rh and Kell) to minimise alloimmunisation. Chelation: start iron chelation after 10-20 units or ferritin >1000 mcg/L."]','A','Pre-transfusion Hb 9-10 g/dL is the key threshold — below this, ineffective erythropoiesis resumes, causing bone disease and splenomegaly. Regular transfusion from diagnosis prevents the deformities seen in untreated thalassaemia major.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Thalassaemia — Clinical Management','intermediate','flashcard','What are the iron chelation agents used in thalassaemia and how are they administered?','["Iron chelation in thalassaemia: 1. Desferrioxamine (deferoxamine) — subcutaneous infusion over 8-12 hours (5-7 nights/week); standard for decades; IV in severe overload; SE: local site reactions, audiological and retinal toxicity (screen annually). 2. Deferasirox (Exjade, Jadenu) — oral once daily; first-line oral agent; SE: GI side effects, renal impairment, hepatotoxicity (monitor renal and hepatic function monthly). 3. Deferiprone — oral three times daily; best for cardiac iron removal; SE: agranulocytosis (weekly FBC mandatory), arthropathy. Combination (deferiprone + desferrioxamine): for severe cardiac iron overload. Monitoring: serum ferritin (target <1000 mcg/L), MRI T2* liver and cardiac (gold standard for organ iron)."]','A','MRI T2* is the gold standard for quantifying iron in liver and heart — ferritin correlates but is less precise. Cardiac iron overload (low T2*) is the most dangerous — causes arrhythmias and cardiac failure, the leading cause of death in thalassaemia.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Thalassaemia — Clinical Management','advanced','flashcard','What are the complications of iron overload in thalassaemia and how are they managed?','["Iron overload complications in thalassaemia: 1. Cardiac: dilated cardiomyopathy, arrhythmias, heart failure — leading cause of death; detected by MRI T2* cardiac (<20 ms = significant). 2. Liver: cirrhosis, hepatocellular carcinoma — liver MRI T2*/liver biopsy for iron quantification. 3. Endocrine: hypogonadotrophic hypogonadism (most common — delayed puberty, amenorrhoea), diabetes mellitus, hypothyroidism, hypoparathyroidism, adrenal insufficiency. 4. Osteoporosis. 5. Skin: bronze pigmentation. Management: aggressive chelation; annual endocrine screening; hormone replacement; DEXA scan; hepatitis B and C vaccination/monitoring (transfusion-related)."]','A','Hypogonadism is the most common endocrine complication — iron deposits in pituitary gonadotrophs. Cardiac iron is the most dangerous — cardiac MRI T2* <10 ms = severe, start intensive chelation (deferiprone preferred for cardiac iron removal).','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Thalassaemia — Clinical Management','advanced','flashcard','What is the curative treatment for thalassaemia major and who are the best candidates?','["Allogeneic stem cell transplantation (SCT) in thalassaemia major: Only curative treatment currently available. Best candidates: 1. Young patients (best results <7 years, before iron overload damage). 2. HLA-matched sibling donor available. 3. Low-risk class (Pesaro classification): absent/mild hepatomegaly, no portal fibrosis, regular chelation compliance. Outcomes: HLA-matched sibling — 80-90% overall survival, 75-85% thalassaemia-free survival; class 3 (non-compliant, significant liver damage) worse outcomes. Complications: GvHD (10-20%), transplant-related mortality (5-10%), graft failure. Gene therapy: autologous HSCs transfected with functional beta-globin gene — clinical trials showing promise (betibeglogene); avoids GvHD and donor requirement."]','A','Pesaro classification (class 1-3) stratifies transplant risk — liver size, portal fibrosis, chelation history. Class 1 = best transplant outcomes. Gene therapy is rapidly advancing and may offer curative treatment without donor dependency.','universal',false,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Thalassaemia — Clinical Management','intermediate','flashcard','How does thalassaemia trait (minor) differ from thalassaemia major clinically and what counselling is required?','["Thalassaemia trait (minor) vs major: Trait (minor): one alpha or one/two beta globin gene defect; clinically ASYMPTOMATIC; mild microcytic hypochromic anaemia (Hb typically 10-12 g/dL); MCV <80 fL; normal or mildly reduced; NOT IDA (iron studies normal/raised ferritin). Often misdiagnosed as IDA — iron therapy will NOT help. Major: both beta-globin genes defective; transfusion-dependent anaemia; diagnosed in first year of life. Counselling: 1. Both partners screen before/during pregnancy if one partner has trait. 2. If both parents trait: 25% chance of major, 50% chance of trait, 25% unaffected. 3. Offer prenatal diagnosis (CVS/amniocentesis) if both carriers."]','A','Thalassaemia trait mimics IDA on indices but iron studies are NORMAL or raised. Key discriminator: Mentzer index = MCV/RBC; <13 suggests thalassaemia; >13 suggests IDA. Hb electrophoresis confirms (raised HbA2 >3.5% in beta-thalassaemia trait).','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Thalassaemia — Clinical Management','intermediate','flashcard','What are the clinical features of untreated or inadequately transfused thalassaemia major?','["Features of inadequately transfused thalassaemia major: Due to MASSIVE ineffective erythropoiesis (bone marrow expansion): 1. Bony deformities: frontal bossing, prominent malar eminences, dental malocclusion, hair-on-end appearance on skull X-ray. 2. Hepatosplenomegaly (extramedullary haematopoiesis). 3. Growth retardation. 4. Severe anaemia (Hb 2-5 g/dL without transfusion). 5. High-output cardiac failure. 6. Pathological fractures (cortical thinning). 7. Bronze skin discolouration (from haemosiderin). 8. Gallstones. Regular transfusion from infancy PREVENTS these complications by suppressing ineffective erythropoiesis."]','A','The bony changes in thalassaemia are caused by medullary expansion trying to compensate for severe anaemia — NOT by iron overload. Adequate transfusion (pre-Hb 9-10 g/dL) from birth prevents bony deformity and splenomegaly.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Thalassaemia — Clinical Management','advanced','flashcard','What are the different types of alpha-thalassaemia and their clinical severity?','["Alpha-thalassaemia spectrum (4 alpha genes): 1. Silent carrier (1 gene deleted, -α/αα): asymptomatic, normal Hb and MCV; no treatment. 2. Alpha-thalassaemia trait (2 genes deleted, --/αα or -α/-α): mild microcytic anaemia; HbA2 normal (contrast beta-trait); no treatment needed. 3. HbH disease (3 genes deleted, --/-α): moderate haemolytic anaemia (Hb 7-10 g/dL); HbH (β4 tetramers); splenomegaly; intermittent transfusions; folic acid; avoid oxidants. 4. Hb Barts hydrops fetalis (4 genes deleted, --/--): incompatible with life (Hb Barts = γ4, no functional Hb); fetal hydrops, stillbirth; if born alive — transfusion dependent from birth; maternal risk (severe pre-eclampsia)."]','A','Alpha-thalassaemia severity = number of deleted genes (1-4). Beta-thalassaemia trait raises HbA2 (>3.5%) on electrophoresis — but alpha-thalassaemia trait has NORMAL HbA2. Hb Barts (gamma tetramers) on newborn screening suggests severe alpha-thalassaemia.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Thalassaemia — Clinical Management','intermediate','flashcard','What is the role of splenectomy in thalassaemia and when is it indicated?','["Splenectomy in thalassaemia: Indications: 1. Increasing transfusion requirement (>200-220 mL/kg/year of packed red cells) suggesting hypersplenism. 2. Massive symptomatic splenomegaly causing pain or discomfort. 3. Hypersplenism causing significant leucopenia or thrombocytopenia. Timing: delay until >6 years old if possible (immune maturity). Pre-splenectomy vaccines mandatory (as per post-splenectomy protocol). Benefits: reduces transfusion requirement, reduces splenic sequestration. Risks: OPSI (2-3% lifetime risk), thrombosis (particularly portal vein thrombosis in thalassaemia), pulmonary hypertension (increased risk post-splenectomy in thalassaemia). Post-splenectomy: lifelong penicillin + 5-year PPSV23 boosters."]','A','Portal vein thrombosis is a specific risk of splenectomy in thalassaemia (higher than other conditions) — mechanism includes altered splenic blood flow and platelet rebound. Anticoagulation may be needed post-operatively.','universal',false,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Thalassaemia — Clinical Management','intermediate','flashcard','How is folic acid used in thalassaemia management?','["Folic acid in thalassaemia: Indication: increased folate demand due to chronic haemolysis and high red cell turnover → risk of superimposed folate deficiency. Dose: folic acid 5 mg orally DAILY. Who requires it: 1. All patients with thalassaemia major (on transfusion programme). 2. Thalassaemia intermedia (non-transfusion dependent). 3. HbH disease. 4. Any patient with significant haemolysis. Not required: thalassaemia trait (asymptomatic minor, no significant haemolysis). Timing: lifelong in transfusion-dependent patients; continue in pregnancy (prevents NTD in addition to folate deficiency). Monitor: serum and red cell folate levels annually."]','A','Chronic haemolysis consumes folate faster than dietary intake can replenish — haemolytic anaemia is a classic cause of superimposed folate deficiency. Daily folic acid supplementation prevents megaloblastic crisis on top of haemolytic anaemia.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Thalassaemia — Clinical Management','advanced','flashcard','What is thalassaemia intermedia and how does its management differ from thalassaemia major?','["Thalassaemia intermedia: Genotype: mild/moderate beta-thalassaemia mutations with some residual beta-globin production; OR co-inheritance with alpha-thalassaemia trait (reduces globin chain imbalance). Clinical: Hb typically 7-10 g/dL without transfusion; splenomegaly; bony changes (milder); can survive without regular transfusion. Management differs from major: 1. Transfusion: intermittent only (acute illness, pregnancy, surgery, pre-transfusion Hb <7 g/dL with symptoms) — NOT regular programme. 2. Folic acid 5mg daily. 3. Hydroxyurea — may raise HbF and reduce symptoms. 4. Iron chelation if ferritin elevated (iron overload occurs from increased GI absorption even without transfusion). 5. Splenectomy if hypersplenism. 6. Allogeneic SCT considered in severe intermedia."]','A','Iron overload in thalassaemia intermedia occurs WITHOUT transfusion — increased iron absorption driven by chronic anaemia and suppressed hepcidin (due to ineffective erythropoiesis). Monitor ferritin even in non-transfused patients and chelate when ferritin >800-1000 mcg/L.','universal',false,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Acute Leukaemia — Clinical Approach','beginner','flashcard','What is tumour lysis syndrome (TLS) and how is it prevented in acute leukaemia?','["Tumour Lysis Syndrome (TLS): Definition: rapid lysis of cancer cells releases intracellular contents → hyperuricaemia, hyperkalaemia, hyperphosphataemia, hypocalcaemia → AKI, arrhythmias, seizures. Risk: highest in ALL, Burkitt lymphoma, AML with high WBC (>50x10⁹/L). Prevention: 1. IV hydration (2-3 L/m²/day) — start 24-48 hours BEFORE chemotherapy. 2. Allopurinol — inhibits xanthine oxidase → reduces uric acid production; start 12-24h before chemo; dose 300mg/day adults. 3. Rasburicase (recombinant urate oxidase) — converts uric acid to allantoin (more soluble); faster acting; use in HIGH RISK or established hyperuricaemia; contraindicated in G6PD deficiency (risk of haemolysis). 4. Monitor electrolytes, uric acid, creatinine, phosphate, calcium every 4-6h."]','A','Allopurinol PREVENTS uric acid formation (blocks xanthine oxidase). Rasburicase BREAKS DOWN existing uric acid — much faster, used in high-risk or established hyperuricaemia. Rasburicase is contraindicated in G6PD deficiency — produces H2O2 which causes haemolysis.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Acute Leukaemia — Clinical Approach','intermediate','flashcard','What is the empirical antibiotic management of neutropenic fever in acute leukaemia?','["Neutropenic fever management: Definition: temperature ≥38.3°C single, or ≥38°C for >1 hour, in a patient with ANC <0.5x10⁹/L (or expected to fall below this). Management — START ANTIBIOTICS WITHIN 1 HOUR: 1. Risk stratify (MASCC score ≥21 = low risk; <21 = high risk). 2. Blood cultures (peripheral + central line) BEFORE antibiotics. 3. Empirical broad-spectrum IV antibiotics: HIGH RISK: piperacillin-tazobactam (Tazocin) 4.5g TDS IV; or meropenem if penicillin allergic or ESBL risk. 4. Add vancomycin/teicoplanin if: central line infection suspected, skin/soft tissue infection, haemodynamic instability, Gram-positive bacteraemia. 5. Add antifungals (caspofungin/voriconazole) if fever persists >4-5 days or fungal risk (prior fungal infection, prolonged neutropenia). 6. G-CSF in severe/refractory cases."]','A','The 1-hour door-to-antibiotic rule in neutropenic fever mirrors sepsis management. Every hour''s delay increases mortality. Blood cultures FIRST but do NOT delay antibiotics beyond culture collection. Tazocin (pip-tazo) is the standard first-line empirical agent.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Acute Leukaemia — Clinical Approach','advanced','flashcard','What is the specific treatment for acute promyelocytic leukaemia (APL) and why is it unique?','["APL (AML M3) treatment: Unique features: APL is caused by t(15;17) translocation → PML-RARα fusion protein → blocks myeloid differentiation. Specific treatment — DO NOT give standard chemotherapy first: 1. All-trans retinoic acid (ATRA) — differentiates APL blasts into mature neutrophils; given immediately on CLINICAL SUSPICION (before cytogenetics confirmed); dose 45 mg/m²/day in divided doses. 2. Arsenic trioxide (ATO) — synergistic with ATRA; standard of care in most APL; combined ATRA+ATO achieves >90% complete remission. 3. Anthracycline chemotherapy (idarubicin) — added in high-risk APL (WBC >10x10⁹/L). Complications: 1. DIC — present in virtually all APL at diagnosis; treat with FFP, cryo, platelets; ATRA reduces DIC. 2. Differentiation syndrome (formerly ATRA syndrome) — respiratory distress, fever, fluid retention; treat with dexamethasone 10mg BD."]','A','APL is the only leukaemia where a targeted non-cytotoxic drug (ATRA) is started BEFORE confirming diagnosis — delay causes death from DIC. APL + DIC = start ATRA now. Differentiation syndrome = dexamethasone + consider interrupting ATRA temporarily.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Acute Leukaemia — Clinical Approach','advanced','flashcard','What is the 7+3 induction chemotherapy regimen for AML and what is its goal?','["7+3 AML induction chemotherapy: Regimen: 1. Cytarabine (Ara-C) — 100-200 mg/m²/day by continuous IV infusion for 7 DAYS. 2. Anthracycline (idarubicin 12mg/m²/day or daunorubicin 60-90 mg/m²/day) IV for 3 DAYS — hence 7+3. Goal: achieve complete remission (CR) — defined as: bone marrow blasts <5%, normal peripheral counts (ANC >1x10⁹/L, platelets >100x10⁹/L), no Auer rods. CR achieved in ~65-80% of patients. After induction: consolidation with high-dose cytarabine (HiDAC) × 3-4 cycles OR allogeneic SCT (for high-risk disease). Response assessment: bone marrow biopsy at day 14-21 (to assess marrow aplasia/residual disease) and at count recovery (~day 28-35)."]','A','7+3 = cytarabine 7 days + anthracycline 3 days. The 14-day bone marrow is a key decision point — persistent blasts at day 14 in recovering marrow = consider re-induction. CR requires blasts <5% AND blood count recovery.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Acute Leukaemia — Clinical Approach','intermediate','flashcard','Why is CNS prophylaxis important in ALL and how is it delivered?','["CNS prophylaxis in ALL: Rationale: ALL blasts frequently invade CNS (blood-brain barrier protects them from systemic chemotherapy) → CNS relapse is common without prophylaxis; CNS is a sanctuary site. Methods: 1. Intrathecal chemotherapy — methotrexate ± cytarabine ± hydrocortisone administered directly into CSF via lumbar puncture; given multiple times throughout induction, consolidation, and maintenance. 2. High-dose systemic methotrexate — penetrates CNS at high doses. 3. Cranial irradiation (24 Gy) — historical standard; now reserved for CNS-positive disease or very high-risk ALL (long-term neurocognitive toxicity limits routine use). 4. Liposomal cytarabine (DepoCyte) intrathecally — for CNS relapse. Paediatric ALL: intrathecal methotrexate alone (no cranial irradiation in standard risk)."]','A','CNS prophylaxis is standard in ALL (not AML, except APL) because ALL blasts infiltrate CSF early and systemic drugs fail to penetrate the blood-brain barrier adequately. Without prophylaxis, CNS relapse risk approaches 30-50%.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Acute Leukaemia — Clinical Approach','beginner','flashcard','What are the presenting clinical features of acute leukaemia?','["Acute leukaemia clinical presentation: Due to bone marrow FAILURE (blasts crowd out normal cells): 1. Anaemia: fatigue, pallor, dyspnoea. 2. Thrombocytopenia: bruising, petechiae, mucosal bleeding, menorrhagia. 3. Neutropenia: recurrent/severe infections, mouth ulcers, perianal sepsis. Due to organ INFILTRATION by blasts: 4. Lymphadenopathy (more ALL than AML). 5. Splenomegaly/hepatomegaly. 6. CNS symptoms (headache, cranial nerve palsies — more ALL). 7. Bone pain (children with ALL). 8. Gum hypertrophy — AML monocytic subtypes (M4/M5). 9. Mediastinal mass — T-cell ALL. Specific: APL presents with DIC (bleeding diathesis); CML blast crisis may mimic AML."]','A','Pancytopenia + blast cells on film = acute leukaemia until proven otherwise. Gum hypertrophy is characteristic of monocytic AML (M4/M5) — monocytes infiltrate gingival tissue. Mediastinal mass in a young person = T-cell ALL until proven otherwise.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Acute Leukaemia — Clinical Approach','intermediate','flashcard','How is ALL distinguished from AML in diagnosis and why does it matter for treatment?','["ALL vs AML distinction: ALL (Acute Lymphoblastic Leukaemia): lymphoid blasts; common in children (peak 2-5 years); treated with multi-agent chemotherapy over 2-3 years including vincristine, L-asparaginase, steroids, intrathecal chemotherapy. AML (Acute Myeloid Leukaemia): myeloid blasts; more common in adults; treated with 7+3 induction. Diagnostic differentiation: 1. Morphology — Auer rods = AML (never ALL). 2. Immunophenotyping (flow cytometry) — definitive; ALL: CD10, CD19 (B-ALL) or CD3, CD5, CD7 (T-ALL); AML: CD13, CD33, CD117, MPO. 3. Cytochemistry — MPO positive = AML. 4. Cytogenetics — t(9;22) Philadelphia chromosome in 25% adult ALL (poor prognosis — add TKI); t(15;17) = APL."]','A','Auer rods on blood film = AML — NEVER seen in ALL. Auer rods are crystallised granules in myeloid blasts. Philadelphia chromosome ALL (t(9;22)) requires addition of imatinib/dasatinib to chemotherapy — dramatically improves prognosis.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Acute Leukaemia — Clinical Approach','advanced','flashcard','What supportive care measures are essential during acute leukaemia induction chemotherapy?','["Supportive care during AML/ALL induction: 1. Infection prevention: reverse barrier nursing (protected environment); antifungal prophylaxis (fluconazole or posaconazole); antiviral prophylaxis (aciclovir for HSV/VZV); antibacterial prophylaxis in some centres (ciprofloxacin); PCP prophylaxis (co-trimoxazole). 2. Transfusion support: platelets threshold typically <10x10⁹/L (or <20 if fever/bleeding); red cells if Hb <7-8 g/dL or symptomatic; irradiated blood products (prevent TA-GvHD in immunocompromised). 3. TLS prevention: IV fluids + allopurinol/rasburicase before/during chemo. 4. Mucositis: oral hygiene, chlorhexidine mouthwash, analgesia, G-CSF may be used. 5. Venous access: central venous catheter (Hickman/PICC) for prolonged infusions."]','A','Irradiated blood products are mandatory in immunocompromised patients (leukaemia, SCT, purine analogues) — non-irradiated products can cause transfusion-associated GvHD (TA-GvHD), where donor lymphocytes attack host tissue — near-universally fatal.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Acute Leukaemia — Clinical Approach','advanced','flashcard','What are the prognostic factors in AML and how do they guide treatment decisions?','["AML prognostic factors and treatment implications: Favourable cytogenetics (standard consolidation — no SCT in CR1): t(15;17) APL, t(8;21) (AML1-ETO), inv(16)/t(16;16) (CBF-AML). Intermediate risk: normal karyotype — NPM1 mutated (without FLT3-ITD) = favourable; FLT3-ITD mutant = poor prognosis; consider SCT in CR1 based on MRD. Poor/adverse risk (allogeneic SCT in CR1 if donor available): complex karyotype (≥3 abnormalities), monosomy 5/7, 17p abnormalities, TP53 mutation, RUNX1, ASXL1 mutations, t(6;9). Age and performance status: older patients (>60) worse outcomes due to higher treatment-related mortality. Targeted therapy: FLT3-ITD → midostaurin or gilteritinib; IDH1/2 mutations → enasidenib/ivosidenib."]','A','Cytogenetics and molecular mutations (NPM1, FLT3, IDH1/2) are the most important prognostic factors in AML and determine whether allogeneic SCT in CR1 is indicated. Favourable risk = chemotherapy alone; adverse risk = aim for SCT.','universal',false,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Acute Leukaemia — Clinical Approach','intermediate','flashcard','What is the initial investigation workup for suspected acute leukaemia?','["Acute leukaemia initial investigations: Urgent: 1. FBC — pancytopenia + circulating blasts (or very high WBC with blasts). 2. Blood film — blasts, Auer rods, blast morphology. 3. Coagulation screen — PT, APTT, fibrinogen (DIC in APL). 4. LDH, uric acid, urea, creatinine, electrolytes, Ca, PO4 — TLS baseline. 5. LFTs, bilirubin. 6. Blood cultures if febrile. 7. Group and screen, crossmatch. 8. ECG (pre-cardiotoxic chemotherapy). Confirmatory: 9. Bone marrow aspirate and trephine biopsy — morphology, immunophenotyping (flow cytometry), cytogenetics (karyotype), molecular (FISH for key translocations, PCR for NPM1/FLT3/BCR-ABL). 10. HLA typing (patient and siblings) — for potential SCT. 11. CXR/CT if mediastinal mass (T-ALL) or infection suspected. 12. LP for CNS assessment in ALL."]','A','NEVER delay starting treatment to wait for all results if patient is sick — start supportive care, TLS prevention, and manage DIC (in APL). Bone marrow aspirate can be taken on day 1; ATRA in APL starts on clinical suspicion.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Lymphoma — Diagnosis & Staging','medium','flashcard','What are the Ann Arbor stages of lymphoma and what defines each stage?','["Stage I: single lymph node region or single extranodal site. Stage II: two or more nodal regions on the SAME side of the diaphragm. Stage III: nodal regions on BOTH sides of the diaphragm. Stage IV: disseminated involvement of extranodal organs (liver, bone marrow, lung). Suffix A = no B symptoms; suffix B = B symptoms present."]','A','Mnemonic: I = one region, II = two or more same side, III = both sides, IV = visceral spread. The diaphragm is the key dividing landmark.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Lymphoma — Diagnosis & Staging','medium','flashcard','What are the B symptoms in lymphoma and why do they matter?','["B symptoms: (1) Unexplained fever >38°C, (2) Drenching night sweats, (3) Unexplained weight loss >10% body weight over 6 months. Their presence upgrades prognosis to a worse category (suffix B) and typically indicates more aggressive disease requiring more intensive treatment."]','A','Mnemonic: FNW — Fever, Night sweats, Weight loss. B symptoms shift Ann Arbor stage from A to B suffix and worsen prognosis in both Hodgkin and non-Hodgkin lymphoma.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Lymphoma — Diagnosis & Staging','medium','flashcard','What is the role of PET-CT in lymphoma management?','["PET-CT (18F-FDG PET combined with CT) is used for: (1) Initial staging — more accurate than CT alone, detects bone marrow involvement, (2) Interim response assessment after 2 cycles of chemotherapy (Deauville score), (3) End-of-treatment response assessment to guide further therapy or consolidation, (4) Detecting relapse. PET-avid lymphomas (Hodgkin, DLBCL, follicular) benefit most."]','A','Deauville 5-point scale: 1-2 = complete metabolic response; 3 = partial response; 4-5 = inadequate response. PET-negativity after 2 cycles of ABVD predicts excellent outcome in Hodgkin lymphoma.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Lymphoma — Diagnosis & Staging','medium','flashcard','Why is excisional lymph node biopsy preferred for lymphoma diagnosis?','["Excisional biopsy (removal of an entire lymph node) is preferred because: (1) Provides intact nodal architecture essential for classifying lymphoma subtype, (2) Allows flow cytometry, immunohistochemistry, FISH, and molecular studies on sufficient tissue, (3) Core needle biopsy may be acceptable but risks sampling error, (4) Fine needle aspiration is INSUFFICIENT — it disrupts architecture and cannot reliably distinguish lymphoma subtypes. Adequate tissue is mandatory for correct classification."]','A','Key principle: lymphoma diagnosis requires architecture. FNA destroys it. Always request the whole node when possible — misclassification leads to wrong treatment.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Lymphoma — Diagnosis & Staging','hard','flashcard','What is R-CHOP and when is it used in lymphoma?','["R-CHOP = Rituximab + Cyclophosphamide + doxorubicin (Hydroxydaunorubicin) + vincristine (Oncovin) + Prednisolone. Standard first-line treatment for Diffuse Large B-Cell Lymphoma (DLBCL). Given in 21-day cycles, typically 6 cycles (R-CHOP 6). Rituximab targets CD20 on B-cells. CHOP without rituximab is used for CD20-negative lymphomas. Achieve ~60-70% cure rate in DLBCL."]','A','Mnemonic: R-CHOP = Rituximab, Cyclo, Hydroxy-dauno, Oncovin, Pred. The R (rituximab) added to CHOP improved overall survival in DLBCL by ~10-15% in the GELA trial.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Lymphoma — Diagnosis & Staging','hard','flashcard','What is ABVD chemotherapy and what is it used for?','["ABVD = Adriamycin (doxorubicin) + Bleomycin + Vinblastine + Dacarbazine. Standard first-line chemotherapy for classical Hodgkin lymphoma (HL). Given in 28-day cycles. Early-stage HL (I-IIA): 2-4 cycles ABVD +/- radiotherapy. Advanced-stage HL (III-IV or B symptoms): 6 cycles ABVD. Bleomycin risk: pulmonary toxicity (monitor for bleomycin lung). Cure rates >85% for early-stage, ~70-80% for advanced-stage HL."]','A','ABVD replaced MOPP due to lower fertility impairment and leukaemia risk. Key bleomycin toxicity: pulmonary fibrosis — monitor lung function and hold bleomycin if pulmonary symptoms develop.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Lymphoma — Diagnosis & Staging','hard','flashcard','When is autologous stem cell transplant (SCT) used in Hodgkin lymphoma?','["Autologous SCT is the standard of care for relapsed or refractory Hodgkin lymphoma (HL) in patients who achieve a second remission with salvage chemotherapy. Indications: (1) First relapse after complete remission, (2) Primary refractory disease responding to salvage therapy. Salvage regimens: DHAP, ICE, ESHAP. High-dose chemotherapy (BEAM: BCNU-etoposide-cytarabine-melphalan) followed by autologous SCT restores haematopoiesis. Long-term remission achieved in ~50% of relapsed HL."]','A','Autologous SCT uses the patient''s own stem cells harvested before high-dose chemo — no graft-versus-host disease risk. Allogeneic SCT reserved for post-autologous relapse.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Lymphoma — Diagnosis & Staging','easy','flashcard','What distinguishes Hodgkin lymphoma from Non-Hodgkin lymphoma histologically?','["Hodgkin lymphoma (HL): characterised by Reed-Sternberg (RS) cells — large binucleate cells with prominent owl-eye nucleoli — on background of reactive inflammatory cells. RS cells are CD15+, CD30+, CD20- (classical HL). Non-Hodgkin lymphoma (NHL): malignant lymphocytes WITHOUT Reed-Sternberg cells. NHL is far more heterogeneous — includes DLBCL, follicular lymphoma, mantle cell, Burkitt, etc. HL spreads in orderly contiguous nodal fashion; NHL spreads unpredictably."]','A','Reed-Sternberg cells are pathognomonic for Hodgkin lymphoma. Owl-eye appearance = two large nucleoli in a giant binucleate cell. CD15/CD30 positive, CD45 negative.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Lymphoma — Diagnosis & Staging','medium','flashcard','What clinical features suggest Hodgkin lymphoma in a young patient?','["Classic presentation: (1) Young adult (bimodal age peak 20s and >55 years), (2) Painless cervical or supraclavicular lymphadenopathy, (3) Mediastinal mass (widened mediastinum on CXR), (4) B symptoms: fever, night sweats, weight loss, (5) Alcohol-induced lymph node pain (pathognomonic but rare), (6) Pruritus. Investigations: FBC (eosinophilia, lymphopenia in advanced disease), ESR elevated, LDH elevated, excisional biopsy showing Reed-Sternberg cells."]','A','Alcohol-induced nodal pain is rare but pathognomonic for Hodgkin lymphoma. Any young adult with persistent cervical adenopathy + B symptoms warrants urgent biopsy.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Lymphoma — Diagnosis & Staging','medium','flashcard','How do you stage lymphoma using the Lugano classification (modified Ann Arbor)?','["Lugano classification (2014, used for PET-avid lymphomas): Stage I: single node or contiguous extranodal site. Stage II: two or more nodal groups same side of diaphragm. Stage II Bulky: Stage II + bulky disease (mass >10cm or >1/3 thoracic diameter). Stage III: nodes both sides of diaphragm or spleen. Stage IV: diffuse extranodal involvement. Key change from Ann Arbor: bone marrow biopsy replaced by PET-CT for FDG-avid lymphomas. A/B suffixes retained."]','A','Lugano updated Ann Arbor for the PET era. Bone marrow biopsy still needed for non-FDG-avid lymphomas (e.g., marginal zone, small lymphocytic lymphoma).','universal',false,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Myeloma — Diagnosis & Management','medium','flashcard','What are the CRAB criteria for multiple myeloma and what does each letter represent?','["CRAB criteria define myeloma-related organ damage requiring treatment: C = Calcium elevated (>2.75 mmol/L or >0.25 above upper limit), R = Renal failure (creatinine >177 micromol/L or eGFR <40 mL/min), A = Anaemia (Hb <100 g/L or >20 g/L below lower limit of normal), B = Bone lytic lesions on imaging (X-ray, CT, MRI, or PET-CT). One or more CRAB features = symptomatic myeloma requiring treatment. Also: SLiM CRAB adds 60% plasma cells, SFLC ratio >100, >1 MRI focal lesion."]','A','CRAB mnemonic: Calcium, Renal, Anaemia, Bone. Any single CRAB criterion justifies starting myeloma treatment regardless of plasma cell percentage.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Myeloma — Diagnosis & Management','medium','flashcard','What is the role of serum free light chains (SFLC) in myeloma diagnosis and monitoring?','["Serum free light chains (kappa and lambda FLC) are immunoglobulin light chains not bound to heavy chains. Role in myeloma: (1) Diagnosis of light chain myeloma and non-secretory myeloma (no M-protein on SPEP), (2) Monitoring response to treatment, (3) Part of SLiM-CRAB criteria — kappa:lambda ratio >100 (with involved FLC >100 mg/L) = myeloma-defining event, (4) International Myeloma Working Group response criteria use SFLC for stringent complete response (sCR). Normal kappa:lambda ratio = 0.26-1.65."]','A','SFLC is particularly valuable when SPEP is negative (light chain only disease). An abnormal FLC ratio >100 or <0.01 is a myeloma-defining event even without CRAB criteria.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Myeloma — Diagnosis & Management','medium','flashcard','What are SPEP and UPEP and what do they detect in myeloma?','["SPEP (Serum Protein Electrophoresis): separates serum proteins by charge/size on gel, detects M-protein (monoclonal paraprotein) as a discrete band in the gamma region. UPEP (Urine Protein Electrophoresis): detects Bence Jones protein (free light chains) in urine — indicates light chain excretion causing renal damage. Both are quantified by immunofixation to identify the immunoglobulin class (IgG, IgA, IgM, kappa, lambda). M-protein >30 g/L = myeloma threshold. Rising M-protein indicates progression."]','A','M-protein on SPEP appears as a narrow spike (monoclonal) vs polyclonal broad band in infection/inflammation. Bence Jones proteins are FLC that pass through glomerulus — nephrotoxic.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Myeloma — Diagnosis & Management','hard','flashcard','What is VMP (Bortezomib-Melphalan-Prednisolone) and when is it used in myeloma?','["VMP = Bortezomib (proteasome inhibitor) + Melphalan (alkylating agent) + Prednisolone. Standard first-line treatment for transplant-ineligible multiple myeloma patients (older/frail patients who cannot receive high-dose chemotherapy). Bortezomib inhibits the 26S proteasome, leading to accumulation of misfolded proteins and apoptosis of plasma cells. Key toxicities: bortezomib — peripheral neuropathy (use subcutaneous route to reduce), herpes zoster reactivation (prophylactic aciclovir required); melphalan — myelosuppression."]','A','VMP is preferred for transplant-ineligible patients. Subcutaneous bortezomib reduces neuropathy vs IV. Always co-prescribe aciclovir 400mg BD as herpes zoster prophylaxis with bortezomib.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Myeloma — Diagnosis & Management','hard','flashcard','What is RVD regimen in myeloma and who receives it?','["RVD = Lenalidomide (Revlimid) + Bortezomib (Velcade) + Dexamethasone. Standard first-line induction regimen for transplant-eligible multiple myeloma patients. Given for 3-4 cycles before stem cell harvest. Lenalidomide is an immunomodulatory drug (IMiD) that activates NK cells and T-cells via cereblon binding, degrading IKZF1/3. Dexamethasone provides anti-myeloma and anti-inflammatory effects. Key toxicities: lenalidomide — DVT/PE (thromboprophylaxis mandatory), cytopenias, teratogenic; bortezomib — neuropathy, infections."]','A','RVD is among the most effective myeloma induction regimens. Lenalidomide requires mandatory pregnancy prevention programme (thalidomide analogue — teratogenic). DVT prophylaxis with aspirin or LMWH required.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Myeloma — Diagnosis & Management','hard','flashcard','What is the role of autologous stem cell transplant (ASCT) in multiple myeloma?','["ASCT is consolidation therapy for transplant-eligible myeloma patients (typically age <70, adequate organ function). Process: (1) Induction chemotherapy (RVD x3-4 cycles) to reduce disease burden, (2) Stem cell mobilisation with G-CSF +/- plerixafor, (3) High-dose melphalan (200 mg/m2) conditioning chemotherapy, (4) Autologous stem cell infusion to rescue bone marrow. Benefits: deepens response (increases CR rates), prolongs progression-free survival (PFS) by ~12 months vs chemotherapy alone. Maintenance lenalidomide post-ASCT further improves PFS and overall survival."]','A','ASCT does not cure myeloma but significantly extends remission. High-dose melphalan (Mel200) is the conditioning backbone. Maintenance lenalidomide post-transplant is now standard of care.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Myeloma — Diagnosis & Management','medium','flashcard','Why and how are bisphosphonates used in multiple myeloma bone disease?','["Bisphosphonates (zoledronic acid or pamidronate) are used in myeloma to: (1) Reduce skeletal-related events (SREs) — fractures, spinal cord compression, need for radiotherapy, (2) Reduce bone pain, (3) Possible anti-myeloma effect (zoledronic acid shown to improve overall survival in MRCIX trial). Mechanism: inhibit osteoclast-mediated bone resorption (myeloma activates osteoclasts via RANKL). Zoledronic acid 4mg IV monthly preferred. Key toxicity: osteonecrosis of the jaw (ONJ) — dental review BEFORE starting; renal impairment — monitor eGFR."]','A','Bisphosphonates prevent the vicious cycle: myeloma cells act via RANKL to activate osteoclasts, causing bone destruction that releases growth factors promoting more myeloma. Dental hygiene essential — ONJ risk with extractions.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Myeloma — Diagnosis & Management','easy','flashcard','What are the diagnostic criteria for multiple myeloma?','["Diagnosis of multiple myeloma requires: Clonal bone marrow plasma cells >=10% OR biopsy-proven plasmacytoma PLUS one or more of: (A) CRAB criteria (Calcium elevated, Renal failure, Anaemia, Bone lesions), OR (B) SLiM criteria — 60% clonal plasma cells, SFLC ratio >=100 (involved:uninvolved), >1 focal lesion on MRI. Monoclonal Gammopathy of Undetermined Significance (MGUS): plasma cells <10%, M-protein <30g/L, no CRAB/SLiM. Smouldering myeloma: plasma cells 10-59%, M-protein >=30g/L, no CRAB/SLiM."]','A','Progression risk: MGUS to smouldering myeloma to myeloma at ~1%/year, ~10%/year, and ~50% at 5 years respectively. SLiM-CRAB added in 2014 to treat high-risk smouldering myeloma earlier.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Myeloma — Diagnosis & Management','medium','flashcard','What investigations are essential in newly diagnosed multiple myeloma?','["Essential workup: Blood — FBC (normocytic anaemia, rouleaux on film), U&E (renal failure), calcium (hypercalcaemia), LDH, beta-2 microglobulin (prognosis), albumin, SPEP + immunofixation, serum free light chains. Urine — 24h UPEP + immunofixation (Bence Jones protein). Bone marrow — trephine biopsy (plasma cell %, clonality, cytogenetics/FISH for del17p, t(4;14), t(14;16) — high-risk features). Imaging — whole-body low-dose CT or PET-CT (preferred over skeletal survey X-rays). Bone marrow FISH guides risk stratification and prognosis."]','A','High-risk cytogenetics: del(17p), t(4;14), t(14;16), gain(1q). These predict poor response and shorter survival — may require bortezomib-based or experimental regimens.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Myeloma — Diagnosis & Management','medium','flashcard','What is the International Staging System (ISS) for myeloma prognosis?','["ISS uses beta-2 microglobulin (B2M) and albumin: Stage I: B2M <3.5 mg/L AND albumin >=35 g/L. Median survival ~62 months. Stage II: Neither stage I nor III. Median survival ~44 months. Stage III: B2M >=5.5 mg/L. Median survival ~29 months. R-ISS (Revised ISS) adds LDH and cytogenetics: R-ISS III = ISS III + high-risk cytogenetics (del17p, t(4;14), t(14;16)) OR elevated LDH. R-ISS is more prognostically accurate. B2M reflects disease burden and renal function; albumin reflects nutritional status."]','A','ISS is simple and widely used. R-ISS improves on it by incorporating cytogenetics. High B2M = high disease burden + often renal impairment. Low albumin = poor nutritional/inflammatory state.','universal',false,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Thrombocytopenia — Clinical Approach','medium','flashcard','What is the pathophysiology of immune thrombocytopenia (ITP) and how is it diagnosed?','["ITP (Immune Thrombocytopenic Purpura): autoantibodies (primarily anti-GPIIb/IIIa) coat platelets and trigger Fc-receptor-mediated phagocytosis by splenic macrophages, leading to platelet destruction. Also: anti-platelet antibodies inhibit megakaryocyte platelet production. Diagnosis is clinical exclusion: isolated thrombocytopenia (low platelets, normal WCC and Hb), no secondary cause (drug-induced, infection — HIV/HCV, SLE, lymphoproliferative disease). Investigations: FBC + film (giant platelets, no fragmented cells), HIV/HCV serology, ANA, bone marrow biopsy if atypical features or age >60."]','A','ITP is a diagnosis of exclusion — no specific confirmatory test. Anti-platelet antibody tests have poor sensitivity/specificity. Key: rule out TTP (schistocytes), drug causes, HIV, HCV.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Thrombocytopenia — Clinical Approach','medium','flashcard','When do you treat ITP and what is the first-line treatment?','["Treatment thresholds in ITP: Platelet >30x10^9/L + no bleeding = observe (no treatment). Platelet 20-30x10^9/L = treat if symptomatic. Platelet <20x10^9/L = most clinicians treat. Platelet <10x10^9/L = always treat. First-line treatment: Prednisolone 1 mg/kg/day orally (or dexamethasone 40 mg daily x4 days) — response in 70-80% but relapse common on tapering. Response typically within 1-2 weeks. Second-line: splenectomy, rituximab, or TPO receptor agonists if relapse/refractory."]','A','The threshold platelet >20k/no treatment guideline reflects that spontaneous serious bleeding is rare above this level. Treatment is for symptomatic or very low counts, not numbers alone.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Thrombocytopenia — Clinical Approach','hard','flashcard','When is IVIG used in ITP and what is its mechanism?','["IVIG in ITP: Indications — emergency situations requiring rapid platelet rise: (1) Severe bleeding (intracranial haemorrhage, major GI bleed), (2) Pre-operative (requires platelet >50-80x10^9/L), (3) Refractory to corticosteroids with active bleeding, (4) Pregnancy (avoids fetal effects of corticosteroids). Mechanism: IVIG saturates Fc receptors on splenic macrophages, blocking opsonised platelet phagocytosis. Dose: 1g/kg IV x2 days or 0.4g/kg x5 days. Response: rapid (24-48h) but temporary (2-4 weeks). Always combine with steroids for sustained response."]','A','IVIG is a bridge therapy — fast but temporary. Used to buy time before surgery or to control acute bleeding while awaiting steroid response. Anti-D immunoglobulin is an alternative in RhD-positive non-splenectomised patients.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Thrombocytopenia — Clinical Approach','hard','flashcard','What are TPO receptor agonists and when are they used in ITP?','["Thrombopoietin receptor agonists (TPO-RAs) stimulate megakaryocyte proliferation and platelet production. Agents: Eltrombopag (oral, 50mg daily) and Romiplostim (subcutaneous weekly). Indications (second-line): (1) Relapsed/refractory ITP after steroids and/or splenectomy, (2) Patients unsuitable for splenectomy. Response rates: ~80% achieve platelet >50x10^9/L. Not curative — platelets fall on stopping. Monitoring: liver function (eltrombopag — hepatotoxic), bone marrow reticulin (romiplostim — risk of fibrosis with long-term use). Thrombosis risk if platelets rise excessively."]','A','TPO-RAs are now used earlier — sometimes before splenectomy. They work even when antibody-mediated destruction is present because they overcome destruction by increasing production. Adjust dose to target platelet 50-200x10^9/L.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Thrombocytopenia — Clinical Approach','hard','flashcard','How do you diagnose and manage Thrombotic Thrombocytopenic Purpura (TTP)?','["TTP: caused by severe ADAMTS13 deficiency (<10% activity) — either acquired (autoantibodies) or congenital (Upshaw-Schulman syndrome). ADAMTS13 normally cleaves ultra-large von Willebrand factor multimers; deficiency leads to platelet-rich microthrombi. Classic pentad: Microangiopathic Haemolytic Anaemia (MAHA) + Thrombocytopenia + Neurological symptoms + Renal failure + Fever. Management: (1) Emergency plasma exchange (PEX) — removes antibodies and replaces ADAMTS13 — MUST start IMMEDIATELY, (2) Corticosteroids, (3) Rituximab for refractory/relapsing TTP, (4) Caplacizumab (anti-VWF) for acute TTP. Do NOT transfuse platelets (worsens thrombosis)."]','A','TTP is a haematological emergency. PLASMIC score helps predict ADAMTS13 <10%. Mortality without plasma exchange = ~90%. Platelet transfusion is CONTRAINDICATED — fuels microthrombosis.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Thrombocytopenia — Clinical Approach','hard','flashcard','What is heparin-induced thrombocytopenia (HIT) and how is it managed?','["HIT is an immune-mediated complication of heparin: IgG antibodies form against heparin-PF4 complexes, activating platelets and endothelium via Fc receptors, causing paradoxical thrombosis. Types: Type I (non-immune, benign, early, resolves) vs Type II (immune-mediated, dangerous). HIT Type II: platelet fall >50% from baseline 5-10 days after heparin (or earlier if prior heparin exposure), plus thrombosis (venous or arterial). Management: (1) STOP all heparin immediately (including flushes), (2) Start non-heparin anticoagulant — argatroban (direct thrombin inhibitor, IV) or danaparoid or fondaparinux, (3) Do NOT use LMWH (cross-reacts). Confirmed by HIT antibody ELISA + serotonin release assay (SRA — gold standard)."]','A','Use the 4T score to assess HIT probability: Thrombocytopenia, Timing, Thrombosis, other causes. Score >=6 = high probability. Never use warfarin acutely in HIT — risk of warfarin-induced skin necrosis (protein C depletion).','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Thrombocytopenia — Clinical Approach','hard','flashcard','What is disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC) and how is it treated?','["DIC: widespread simultaneous activation of coagulation AND fibrinolysis causing microthrombi (organ damage) AND consumption of clotting factors/platelets leading to bleeding. Causes: sepsis (commonest), obstetric emergencies (abruption, AFE, HELLP), massive trauma, malignancy (APML, mucin-secreting adenocarcinoma), transfusion reactions. Labs: low platelets, prolonged PT/APTT, low fibrinogen (<1.5 g/L), high D-dimer/fibrin degradation products, low factors V/VIII. Management: (1) TREAT UNDERLYING CAUSE — essential, (2) FFP (replenishes clotting factors), (3) Cryoprecipitate (high fibrinogen + factor VIII), (4) Platelet transfusion if <50x10^9/L + bleeding, (5) Tranexamic acid if hyperfibrinolysis predominates."]','A','DIC is a consumptive coagulopathy — you cannot replace fast enough without treating the trigger. APML-associated DIC: ATRA (all-trans retinoic acid) is the specific treatment — start before chemotherapy.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Thrombocytopenia — Clinical Approach','easy','flashcard','How do you differentiate thrombocytopenia causes using the blood film?','["Blood film findings guide thrombocytopenia aetiology: (1) Schistocytes (fragmented RBCs) = microangiopathic haemolytic anaemia — TTP, HUS, DIC, malignant hypertension. (2) Giant platelets = peripheral destruction (ITP) or inherited thrombocytopenia (Bernard-Soulier). (3) Hypersegmented neutrophils + oval macrocytes = B12/folate deficiency (pancytopenia). (4) Blasts = leukaemia or bone marrow failure. (5) Rouleaux = myeloma. (6) Target cells = liver disease, hyposplenism. (7) Normal film with isolated low platelets = ITP, drug-induced, or viral (HIV, EBV, CMV)."]','A','Blood film is the single most useful investigation in thrombocytopenia — never skip it. Schistocytes in the right clinical context = TTP until proven otherwise = plasma exchange urgently.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Thrombocytopenia — Clinical Approach','medium','flashcard','What is haemolytic uraemic syndrome (HUS) and how does it differ from TTP?','["HUS: triad of MAHA + thrombocytopenia + acute kidney injury (AKI predominates). Typical HUS (90%): Shiga-toxin producing E. coli O157:H7 (STEC-HUS) — bloody diarrhoea prodrome in children; kidney most affected. Atypical HUS (aHUS): complement dysregulation (mutations in CFH, CFI, MCP) — recurrent, no diarrhoea prodrome. TTP vs HUS: TTP — ADAMTS13 <10%, neurological symptoms predominate, renal involvement milder; HUS — ADAMTS13 normal, AKI predominates, neurological less common. Management: STEC-HUS — supportive (no antibiotics — worsen toxin release); aHUS — eculizumab (anti-C5 complement inhibitor)."]','A','Key distinction: TTP = ADAMTS13 deficiency + neurological predominance; HUS = normal ADAMTS13 + severe AKI. Bloody diarrhoea in a child with AKI + low platelets = STEC-HUS. Avoid antibiotics in STEC-HUS.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Thrombocytopenia — Clinical Approach','medium','flashcard','What is the approach to thrombocytopenia in pregnancy?','["Thrombocytopenia in pregnancy causes: (1) Gestational thrombocytopenia (GT) — commonest, mild (platelet 70-150x10^9/L), late pregnancy, benign, resolves postpartum, no treatment required, (2) ITP — may pre-exist or present in pregnancy, platelet often <50x10^9/L, treat with prednisolone or IVIG, target platelet >50x10^9/L for vaginal delivery or >80x10^9/L for caesarean/epidural, (3) HELLP syndrome — Haemolysis, Elevated Liver enzymes, Low Platelets — complication of pre-eclampsia, delivery is definitive treatment, (4) TTP/HUS — rare, urgent plasma exchange for TTP. Key: ITP antibodies cross placenta — check fetal platelet count at delivery."]','A','Gestational thrombocytopenia: platelet >70x10^9/L, no prior ITP history, resolves within 6 weeks postpartum — no investigation or treatment needed. ITP: platelet often <50x10^9/L, requires treatment if symptomatic or pre-procedure.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Anticoagulation Management','medium','flashcard','What are the DOAC options for DVT/PE treatment and for how long should anticoagulation be continued?','["DOACs (Direct Oral Anticoagulants) are first-line for DVT and PE (preferred over LMWH/warfarin in most patients). Options: (1) Rivaroxaban 15mg BD x21 days then 20mg OD, (2) Apixaban 10mg BD x7 days then 5mg BD, (3) Dabigatran (after 5-10 days LMWH lead-in) 150mg BD, (4) Edoxaban (after LMWH lead-in). Duration: Provoked VTE (surgery, immobility, trauma) = 3 months. Unprovoked VTE = minimum 3 months, consider 6 months or indefinite based on bleeding vs recurrence risk. Cancer-associated VTE = LMWH or DOAC (rivaroxaban/apixaban preferred) indefinitely while cancer active."]','A','DOAC advantages: oral, no monitoring, fewer interactions, non-inferior to warfarin. Key exception: antiphospholipid syndrome — warfarin preferred (DOACs inferior in triple-positive APS).','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Anticoagulation Management','medium','flashcard','What is the CHA2DS2-VASc score and how is it used in atrial fibrillation?','["CHA2DS2-VASc scores stroke risk in non-valvular AF: C = Congestive heart failure (1), H = Hypertension (1), A2 = Age >=75 (2), D = Diabetes mellitus (1), S2 = Stroke/TIA history (2), V = Vascular disease — MI/PAD (1), A = Age 65-74 (1), Sc = Sex category female (1). Maximum 9 points. Anticoagulation: Men >=2 points = anticoagulate; Women >=3 points = anticoagulate. Men with score 1 or women with score 2 = consider anticoagulation. Score 0 (men) or 1 (women) = no anticoagulation. DOAC preferred over warfarin for non-valvular AF."]','A','Mnemonic: CHA2DS2-VASc. The 2s are for age >=75 and stroke history — these are the highest-weight risk factors. Female sex alone (score=1) does not mandate anticoagulation.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Anticoagulation Management','hard','flashcard','Why must patients with mechanical prosthetic heart valves receive warfarin rather than DOACs?','["Mechanical prosthetic heart valves require warfarin (vitamin K antagonist) for anticoagulation because: (1) DOACs were shown to be INFERIOR to warfarin in the RE-ALIGN trial (dabigatran) — increased stroke and valve thrombosis rates, (2) The thrombogenic surface of mechanical valves may require sustained high-level anticoagulation that DOACs do not reliably provide, (3) No DOAC is licensed for mechanical valves. Target INR: Aortic mechanical valve = 2.5 (range 2.0-3.0). Mitral mechanical valve = 3.0 (range 2.5-3.5). Higher INR for mitral due to lower-velocity, higher-risk flow. Bioprosthetic valves: anticoagulation for 3 months post-insertion then antiplatelet."]','A','RE-ALIGN trial stopped early due to harm with dabigatran in mechanical valves. All DOACs are contraindicated for mechanical heart valves — use warfarin only.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Anticoagulation Management','hard','flashcard','What is the bridging anticoagulation protocol when a patient on warfarin needs surgery?','["Bridging anticoagulation for peri-operative warfarin management: (1) Assess thrombosis risk (high risk = mechanical mitral valve, AF with CHA2DS2-VASc >=5, VTE within 3 months = bridge; low risk = stop warfarin, no bridge), (2) Stop warfarin 5 days before surgery (INR falls to <1.5 in most patients), (3) Start therapeutic LMWH (bridging) when INR <2.0 (typically day 3), (4) Last dose LMWH 24h before surgery (therapeutic LMWH) or 12h (prophylactic LMWH), (5) Resume warfarin 12-24 hours post-operatively when haemostasis achieved, (6) Resume LMWH 48-72h post-op until INR therapeutic."]','A','BRIDGE trial showed bridging did NOT reduce arterial thromboembolism in AF patients and INCREASED bleeding — so bridge only truly high-risk patients (mechanical valves, recent VTE/stroke). Check INR day of surgery.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Anticoagulation Management','hard','flashcard','How do you reverse anticoagulation in major bleeding?','["Reversal agents by anticoagulant: (1) Warfarin — Vitamin K (5-10mg IV, slow reversal 6-12h) + Prothrombin Complex Concentrate (PCC 25-50 IU/kg, immediate reversal) +/- FFP. (2) Dabigatran (direct thrombin inhibitor) — Idarucizumab (Praxbind) 5g IV, specific antidote, immediate reversal. (3) Rivaroxaban/Apixaban/Edoxaban (Factor Xa inhibitors) — Andexanet alfa (specific antidote, IV bolus + infusion) or PCC 50 IU/kg if andexanet unavailable. (4) LMWH — Protamine sulphate (1mg per 100 units anti-Xa, max 50mg IV). (5) UFH — Protamine sulphate (1mg per 100 units UFH given in last 2-3h)."]','A','Mnemonic reversal: Warfarin = Vit K + PCC, Dabigatran = idarucizumab, Xa inhibitors = andexanet alfa, Heparin = protamine. Idarucizumab: humanised antibody fragment with 350x affinity for dabigatran vs thrombin.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Anticoagulation Management','medium','flashcard','What is the HAS-BLED score and how does it inform anticoagulation decisions in AF?','["HAS-BLED assesses major bleeding risk in anticoagulated AF patients: H = Hypertension (uncontrolled, SBP >160 mmHg) — 1 point. A = Abnormal renal/liver function (each 1 point, max 2). S = Stroke history — 1 point. B = Bleeding history or predisposition — 1 point. L = Labile INR (TTR <60%) — 1 point. E = Elderly (age >65) — 1 point. D = Drugs (antiplatelets/NSAIDs) or alcohol (each 1 point, max 2). Score >=3 = high bleeding risk — warrants caution, more frequent review, but does NOT contraindicate anticoagulation if stroke risk high. Modify correctable risk factors."]','A','HAS-BLED is used to identify and correct bleeding risks, NOT to withhold anticoagulation. High HAS-BLED + high CHA2DS2-VASc = still anticoagulate but address modifiable factors (control BP, avoid NSAIDs).','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Anticoagulation Management','medium','flashcard','What are the differences between unfractionated heparin (UFH) and low molecular weight heparin (LMWH)?','["UFH vs LMWH: UFH — large molecule, binds antithrombin III, inhibits IIa AND Xa (anti-Xa:IIa ratio 1:1), monitored by APTT, IV infusion or SC, fully reversed by protamine, used when rapid reversal needed (surgery, renal failure eGFR <30). LMWH (enoxaparin, dalteparin, tinzaparin) — smaller molecule, predominantly anti-Xa activity (anti-Xa:IIa 2-4:1), predictable pharmacokinetics, SC once or twice daily, no routine monitoring (check anti-Xa levels in renal impairment/obesity/pregnancy), partial reversal by protamine (~60%), dose-adjust in renal impairment. LMWH preferred for most clinical situations."]','A','Key clinical point: in renal failure (eGFR <30), LMWH accumulates — use UFH instead (or reduce LMWH dose with anti-Xa monitoring). Tinzaparin has least renal accumulation among LMWHs.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Anticoagulation Management','easy','flashcard','What are the indications for indefinite anticoagulation in venous thromboembolism?','["Indications for indefinite anticoagulation after VTE: (1) Unprovoked PE (high recurrence risk ~30% at 5 years without anticoagulation), (2) Recurrent VTE (>=2 episodes), (3) Active cancer (ongoing VTE risk while cancer active), (4) Antiphospholipid syndrome (high recurrence risk), (5) Major inherited thrombophilia — antithrombin deficiency, protein C/S deficiency, homozygous Factor V Leiden, compound heterozygous (FVL + prothrombin mutation), (6) Unprovoked proximal DVT. Annual reassessment: reassess bleeding vs recurrence risk yearly and patient preference. DOAC preferred for indefinite therapy (reduced dose apixaban 2.5mg BD after 6 months)."]','A','Reduced-dose DOACs for extended therapy: rivaroxaban 10mg OD or apixaban 2.5mg BD after 6 months reduces VTE recurrence with lower bleeding risk vs therapeutic dose.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Anticoagulation Management','medium','flashcard','How do you manage anticoagulation in pregnancy?','["Anticoagulation in pregnancy: DOACs are CONTRAINDICATED (teratogenic, cross placenta, no safety data). Warfarin is CONTRAINDICATED in first trimester (embryopathy — nasal hypoplasia, stippled epiphyses at 6-9 weeks) and near delivery (neonatal bleeding). Safe option: LMWH throughout pregnancy (does not cross placenta). Protocol: LMWH therapeutic dosing (enoxaparin 1mg/kg BD) throughout pregnancy, switch to UFH infusion at 36 weeks (shorter half-life, reversible with protamine at delivery), restart LMWH 4-6h post-vaginal delivery or 12-24h post-caesarean. Warfarin safe in second trimester for mechanical heart valves (lowest thrombosis risk)."]','A','LMWH is the backbone of anticoagulation in pregnancy. Exception: mechanical heart valves in 2nd trimester — warfarin may be used (highest thrombosis risk outweighs embryopathy risk). Always check anti-Xa levels in pregnancy (altered pharmacokinetics).','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Anticoagulation Management','medium','flashcard','What is thrombophilia screening and when should it be performed after VTE?','["Thrombophilia screening tests: Inherited — Factor V Leiden (APC resistance), Prothrombin G20210A mutation, Antithrombin deficiency, Protein C deficiency, Protein S deficiency. Acquired — Antiphospholipid syndrome (lupus anticoagulant, anti-cardiolipin antibodies, anti-beta2-glycoprotein I antibodies — must be positive on 2 occasions 12 weeks apart). When to test: Unprovoked VTE age <50, recurrent VTE, VTE at unusual sites (cerebral vein, mesenteric, portal), strong family history, pregnancy-associated VTE. When NOT to test: Provoked VTE (major surgery/trauma), warfarin therapy (affects protein C/S/antithrombin), acute thrombosis. Test 3 months after completing anticoagulation when off warfarin for 4 weeks (DOACs affect lupus anticoagulant assay)."]','A','Timing matters: protein C and S are reduced by warfarin and acute thrombosis. Test off anticoagulation. Antiphospholipid syndrome requires 2 positive tests 12 weeks apart — single positive is insufficient for diagnosis.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Blood Transfusion — Clinical Practice','medium','flashcard','What are the indications for red cell transfusion in clinical practice?','["Red cell transfusion indications: (1) Symptomatic anaemia (fatigue, dyspnoea, angina) with Hb <70-80 g/L (70 g/L = general threshold; 80 g/L for cardiac disease or post-cardiac surgery). (2) Acute haemorrhage: transfuse to maintain Hb >70-80 g/L (NICE: restrictive transfusion improves outcomes). (3) Pre-operative: consider if Hb <80 g/L before major surgery. (4) Haemoglobinopathies (sickle cell, thalassaemia): disease-specific protocols. (5) Chronic bone marrow failure if symptomatic. Restrictive strategy (Hb 70 g/L trigger) = non-inferior to liberal (Hb 100 g/L) in most patients + fewer complications. Each unit RBC raises Hb by ~10 g/L."]','A','TRICC trial: Hb 70 g/L transfusion trigger non-inferior to 100 g/L in ICU (actually better in younger, less sick patients). Restrictive transfusion = fewer infections, shorter hospital stay.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Blood Transfusion — Clinical Practice','medium','flashcard','What is ABO and Rhesus compatibility in blood transfusion and why does it matter?','["ABO system: A, B, AB, O blood groups determined by antigens on RBC surface and naturally occurring antibodies in plasma. O negative = universal donor (no A, B, or Rh antigens). AB positive = universal recipient. Incompatible ABO transfusion triggers acute haemolytic transfusion reaction (complement activation, intravascular haemolysis) which is potentially fatal. Rhesus (Rh) system: D antigen most important. Rh-negative patients must not receive Rh-positive blood (risk of alloimmunisation causing haemolytic disease of the fetus/newborn in subsequent pregnancies). Pre-transfusion testing: ABO/Rh typing + antibody screen + cross-match (electronic or serological)."]','A','Group O RhD-negative blood is used in emergencies before cross-match available (O neg packed cells). In major haemorrhage, release O neg immediately — do not wait for cross-match.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Blood Transfusion — Clinical Practice','hard','flashcard','What are the features and management of an acute haemolytic transfusion reaction (AHTR)?','["AHTR: caused by ABO-incompatible transfusion (usually clerical error). Onset: during transfusion or within minutes-hours. Features: fever, rigors, agitation, back/loin pain, chest pain, hypotension, haemoglobinuria (red/brown urine), DIC, acute kidney injury. In anaesthetised patients: unexplained hypotension, haemoglobinuria, DIC. Management: (1) STOP transfusion IMMEDIATELY, (2) Maintain IV access with normal saline, (3) Alert blood bank — check patient identity and blood pack compatibility, (4) Send blood for repeat group/cross-match, DAT (direct antiglobulin test), FBC, U&E, coagulation, blood cultures, (5) Aggressive fluid resuscitation to protect kidneys, (6) Monitor urine output (target >1 mL/kg/h), (7) Treat DIC and shock, (8) Urgent haematology review."]','A','STOP transfusion is the FIRST action — before any investigation. The severity correlates with volume transfused. ABO-incompatibility kills via complement-mediated intravascular haemolysis leading to DIC and renal failure.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Blood Transfusion — Clinical Practice','medium','flashcard','What is a febrile non-haemolytic transfusion reaction (FNHTR) and how is it managed?','["FNHTR: commonest transfusion reaction. Caused by recipient antibodies against donor leukocyte antigens, or cytokines in stored blood products. Features: temperature rise >=1°C above baseline, chills, rigors, headache during or shortly after transfusion. No haemolysis. Management: (1) Slow the transfusion rate or temporarily stop, (2) Administer paracetamol 1g orally/IV, (3) Monitor closely — if fever persists or other symptoms develop, STOP transfusion and exclude haemolytic reaction (ABO incompatibility) or bacterial contamination, (4) If mild/settled, may cautiously restart at slower rate, (5) Pre-medicate with paracetamol for future transfusions if recurrent. Leukodepletion of blood products (standard in UK since 1999) has significantly reduced FNHTR incidence."]','A','FNHTR vs AHTR: both cause fever but FNHTR has NO haemolysis (no haemoglobinuria, no DIC, no hypotension, no loin pain). Always check for haemolysis before labelling as FNHTR.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Blood Transfusion — Clinical Practice','hard','flashcard','What is Transfusion-Related Acute Lung Injury (TRALI) and how is it managed?','["TRALI: life-threatening transfusion complication. Pathophysiology: donor anti-HLA or anti-neutrophil antibodies activate recipient neutrophils in pulmonary vasculature, causing endothelial damage and non-cardiogenic pulmonary oedema. Timing: within 6 hours of transfusion (usually 1-2h). Features: acute dyspnoea, hypoxia (SpO2 <90%), bilateral infiltrates on CXR (white-out), fever, hypotension, NO evidence of circulatory overload (normal or low CVP, normal BNP). Management: (1) STOP transfusion immediately, (2) High-flow O2, (3) Supportive care in ICU, (4) Mechanical ventilation if required (lung-protective strategy), (5) Do NOT give diuretics (not fluid overload), (6) Report to blood bank — implicated unit withdrawn, donor investigated. Mortality ~5-10%."]','A','TRALI vs TACO: TRALI = non-cardiogenic (low/normal BNP, no fluid overload); TACO = cardiogenic (high BNP, fluid overload, responds to diuretics). Both cause pulmonary oedema post-transfusion but treatment is opposite — do not give diuretics for TRALI.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Blood Transfusion — Clinical Practice','hard','flashcard','What is Transfusion-Associated Circulatory Overload (TACO) and how is it distinguished from TRALI?','["TACO: hydrostatic pulmonary oedema from volume overload during transfusion. Risk factors: elderly, pre-existing heart failure, renal failure, rapid transfusion rate. Features: dyspnoea, hypertension, tachycardia, raised JVP, bilateral basal crackles, bilateral infiltrates on CXR, hypoxia — typically within 6h (often during transfusion). BNP/NT-proBNP elevated (differentiates from TRALI). Management: (1) STOP or slow transfusion, (2) Sit patient upright, (3) Furosemide 40-80mg IV, (4) O2, (5) Consider slowing future transfusions + pre-treat with furosemide. Prevention: transfuse 1 unit at a time, reassess before next unit, furosemide between units in high-risk patients."]','A','TACO is the most common serious transfusion complication causing death/serious harm in the UK (SHOT data). Key differentiator from TRALI: raised BNP, hypertension, raised JVP, responds to diuretics.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Blood Transfusion — Clinical Practice','medium','flashcard','What blood products are available and what are their specific indications?','["Blood products and indications: (1) Packed Red Blood Cells (pRBCs) — symptomatic anaemia, acute haemorrhage. (2) Fresh Frozen Plasma (FFP) — coagulation factor deficiency, DIC, TTP (as replacement in plasma exchange), reversal of warfarin when PCC unavailable, massive transfusion. (3) Cryoprecipitate — fibrinogen replacement (fibrinogen <1.5-2 g/L), DIC, haemophilia A (factor VIII), von Willebrand disease. (4) Platelets — thrombocytopenia with bleeding or <10x10^9/L, pre-procedure if <50x10^9/L. (5) Prothrombin Complex Concentrate (PCC) — warfarin reversal. (6) Albumin — cirrhotic paracentesis, SBP, hepatorenal syndrome. (7) Anti-D immunoglobulin — RhD-negative women post-sensitising event."]','A','Cryoprecipitate is precipitate from thawed FFP — concentrated fibrinogen, factor VIII, vWF, factor XIII. Used specifically when fibrinogen low. FFP dilutes fibrinogen — cryoprecipitate is better for fibrinogen replacement.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Blood Transfusion — Clinical Practice','medium','flashcard','What is the major haemorrhage protocol (1:1:1 ratio) in massive transfusion?','["Major haemorrhage protocol (MHP) is activated when patient has or is expected to have life-threatening haemorrhage. MHP pack: ratio-based transfusion using 1:1:1 ratio — 1 unit FFP : 1 unit platelets : 1 unit RBCs (replicates whole blood). Evidence: PROPPR trial showed 1:1:1 improved haemostasis and 24h survival vs 1:1:2 (less FFP). Additional measures: (1) Tranexamic acid 1g IV within 3h of injury (CRASH-2 trial — reduces mortality if given early, harmful if >3h), (2) Calcium gluconate (citrate in blood products chelates ionised calcium causing hypocalcaemia and cardiac dysfunction), (3) Fibrinogen concentrate or cryoprecipitate if fibrinogen <1.5g/L, (4) Warm blood products (prevent hypothermia), (5) Point-of-care coagulation monitoring (TEG/ROTEM)."]','A','Death from massive haemorrhage = lethal triad: hypothermia + acidosis + coagulopathy. MHP addresses coagulopathy. Tranexamic acid is cheap, effective, must be given within 3 hours — becomes harmful after 3h (CRASH-2).','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Blood Transfusion — Clinical Practice','easy','flashcard','What are the mandatory checks before administering a blood transfusion?','["Pre-transfusion safety checks (MANDATORY — most transfusion errors are clerical): (1) Confirm patient identity: full name, date of birth, hospital number — ask patient to state their name and DOB, check wristband, (2) Check blood pack label matches patient identity EXACTLY on wristband and compatibility report, (3) Confirm blood group compatibility on the transfusion label, (4) Check expiry date of blood product, (5) Inspect the blood unit — no clumps, discolouration, or leaks, (6) Confirm prescription: correct component, correct dose, correct rate, (7) Two-person check recommended for identity. Document: start time, finish time, pack number, observations before/during/after transfusion every 15-30 minutes for first hour."]','A','The majority of haemolytic transfusion deaths result from clerical errors — wrong blood given to wrong patient. NEVER transfuse blood without completing identity checks at the bedside with the patient.','universal',true,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Haematology','Clinicals','Blood Transfusion — Clinical Practice','medium','flashcard','What is transfusion-associated graft-versus-host disease (TA-GvHD) and how is it prevented?','["TA-GvHD: rare but almost universally fatal transfusion complication. Mechanism: viable donor T-lymphocytes engraft in immunocompromised recipient and attack host tissues (skin, gut, liver, bone marrow). Features 1-2 weeks post-transfusion: erythematous rash, diarrhoea, hepatitis, pancytopenia (bone marrow failure — distinguishes from typical GvHD). Risk groups: (1) Severely immunocompromised patients (SCT recipients, congenital immunodeficiency, lymphoma on chemotherapy), (2) Immunocompetent patients receiving blood from HLA-homozygous donors (e.g., directed donations from relatives). Prevention: irradiation of cellular blood products for at-risk patients — gamma irradiation destroys T-lymphocyte proliferative capacity. Also: pathogen-reduced products."]','A','TA-GvHD carries ~90% mortality — no effective treatment. Prevention is critical: irradiated blood products for all patients post-allogeneic SCT, lymphoma treatment, congenital immunodeficiency. Irradiation does not affect RBC or platelet function.','universal',false,'{"year1","year2","year3","year4","year5","year6","practitioner"}','Harrison''s Principles of Internal Medicine 20th Ed');